WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:18.519
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs, this is Tim Ferriss, and welcome

00:00:18.519 --> 00:00:22.600
to another episode of The Tim Ferriss Show,
where it is my job to deconstruct world-class

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.800
performers from all different disciplines.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.359
My guest today is Shirley A. Sahrmann, PT,
PhD.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:37.000
But before we get to her bio, let me just
explain: Shirley is a legend in the physical

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.260
therapy world.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.489
She has influenced some of the top performance
coaches in the world.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:48.980
She’s also 85 years old, going on 86, and
is not only razor-sharp mentally but in excellent

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.540
shape physically.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.510
So she walks the walk.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.600
So let me get to the bio, and I will also
give you a bit of 101 on some of the terms

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.550
that we’ll use in the conversation.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.409
Shirley A. Sahrmann, PT, PhD, is Professor
Emerita of Physical Therapy at Washington

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:07.340
University School of Medicine in St. Louis,
Missouri.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.250
She received her bachelor’s degree in physical
therapy and her masters and doctorate degrees

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.600
in neurobiology from Washington University,
where she joined the physical therapy faculty

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.320
and became the first director of their PhD
program in movement science.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.729
Shirley—and she asked me to call her Shirley—became
a Catherine Worthingham Fellow of the American

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:30.610
Physical Therapy Association in 1986 and in
1998 was selected to receive the Mary McMillan

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.740
Award, the Association’s highest honor.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.939
She has also received Washington University’s
Distinguished Faculty Award, the Distinguished

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:42.969
Alumni Award, the School of Medicine’s Inaugural
Distinguished Clinician Award, and an honorary

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:45.330
doctorate from the University of Indianapolis.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:51.200
She has received, as well, the Bowling-Erhard
Orthopedic Clinical Practice Award from the

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.210
Orthopaedic Academy of the APTA.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.860
She has served on the APTA Board of Directors
and as president of the Missouri Chapter.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.719
Her books are iconic.

00:02:01.719 --> 00:02:09.179
They have been the initial domino that has
toppled over, so to speak, the enthusiasm,

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:15.390
the ignition, for many people to get into
the field of movement science and physical

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.970
therapy and performance coaching.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.310
Her first book, Diagnosis and Treatment of
Movement Impairment Syndromes—you may have

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.150
heard Eric Cressey mention this; it was a
hugely influential book for him—has been

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.800
translated into seven languages.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.980
Her second book, Movement System Impairment
Syndromes of the Cervical and Thoracic Spines

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:36.129
and the Extremities, has been equally influential
in promoting movement diagnoses.

00:02:36.129 --> 00:02:40.620
And in this episode, we do a deep dive on
low-back pain, and that is something for the

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.700
first time really I have been struggling with
for the last, let’s just call it nine months

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.700
or so.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.040
Could be a bit longer.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.850
And we do get into the weeds with regard to
anatomy, particularly as it relates to the

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.440
back stuff that I mentioned.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.510
So I’d like to go over a few terms before
we start the interview so you don’t have

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.500
to wonder what they are and feel like you
need to pause to look them up.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.580
You can get through the interview without
listening to my definitions, but some of them

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:06.580
might be helpful.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.730
Also, if you’re a kinesiologist or professional,
please excuse these very simplistic and possibly

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.209
slightly off definitions, but they’ll help
a lot of people.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:16.180
So the first one is the iliac crest.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.180
What is that?

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.760
That’s the uppermost border of your pelvic
girdle.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.680
So you can think of the pelvic girdle as that
large boney bowl that you see in the middle

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.689
of a skeleton hanging in a science classroom
or something.

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:31.170
You can feel your iliac crest if you press
your thumb into the top of your hip.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.900
That sort of boney ridge is your iliac crest.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.370
The tensor fasciae latae— And I’ve heard
many different pronunciations of this, and

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.420
the fact of the matter is no one really speaks
Latin correctly because we don’t know if

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:52.440
it was, you know, veni, vidi, vici, or weni,
widi, wici, for those who get that reference—I

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.280
came, I saw, I conquered.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.540
So anyway, it is better known and abbreviated
as the TFL for a lot of people.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.459
So the TFL is a muscle at the outside, very
outside, portion of the thigh, at the very

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:04.459
top.

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:06.439
So you can think of it all— So people think
of it as a hip muscle sometimes.

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:10.540
You use it to balance your pelvis when standing,
walking, or running.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:16.070
If you ever give someone a piggyback ride,
and a muscle gets super sore on the side of

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:19.850
your hip, that is probably, at least including,
the TFL.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:25.280
You also use the TFL for abducting your hip
or pulling it away from the midline of your

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.860
body, compared to adducting, with two d’s,
which would be pulling it toward the midline.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.570
One way that I remember that—abduction is
like an alien abduction, taking you away,

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:41.449
so moving the, say, leg away from the body,
abducting; bringing the thigh in, adduction,

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:46.360
so like an adductor machine at a gym, one
of those Suzanne Somers thighmaster-type machines

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.450
at the gym would be that.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:51.479
We also talk about muscles that assist in
lateral rotation.

00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:54.270
Lateral rotation is rotating away from the
center of the body.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.400
Medial rotation is rotating towards the center.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.919
So imagine if you turned your feet outwards
to look like you’re duck-footed, that would

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:03.539
be lateral rotation.

00:05:03.539 --> 00:05:04.820
Out to the sides.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:11.350
And then if you turn them inward to be pigeon-toed,
that would be medial rotation of both femurs

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.350
inward.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.480
All right, psoas major I also mention psoas
major.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.020
That’s spelled P-S-O-A-S major.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:22.639
That is a large muscle that joins the upper
and lower parts of the body.

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:25.009
And it also contributes to a lot of lower-back
pain.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:28.080
It connects to the inside of the lower back.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:35.639
And if you were to take, say, your four fingers,
and move them four inches to either side of

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:42.150
your navel and then press in, say, four inches,
that would probably touch your psoas major,

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.360
which is why massage therapy that addresses
it can be so uncomfortable.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.770
For you chefs out there or hunters or people
who might recognize this, this would be the

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:52.479
equivalent of your tenderloin.

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:58.460
So if you’re wondering what a tenderloin
is, it is this muscle in many animals.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:03.389
There may be other ways to use that butchering
term, but psoas major/tenderloin.

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:06.919
There you have it, used mostly for posture
and so on.

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:11.080
We also get into stenosis as it relates to
my spine.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.200
So stenosis is an abnormal narrowing.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:20.620
And I have some stenosis around L4, L5, which
is in the lumbar spine, or lower spine, which

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.830
puts pressure on some nerves there and causes
all sorts of pain.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:30.490
The thoracic spine is more of, say, the middle
of the back, and you just think between the

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.360
shoulder blades for simplicity.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.930
OK, last, and I could say not least, but who
knows.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.220
These are all kind of equivalent and useful.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.599
You have supine vs. prone positions.

00:06:41.599 --> 00:06:43.259
Supine is lying on your back.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:44.389
Prone is lying on your stomach.

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:50.889
If you’ve ever wondered what a supinated
grip is or a pronated grip, supinated is palm

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:51.889
up.

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:54.960
And you can remember that because if you want
to pour soup into your hand, you have your

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.190
palm up.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.360
OK, so that is supine.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.259
And honestly, learning the basics of anatomy
and the basics of some medical terminology

00:07:04.259 --> 00:07:08.990
is, I think, one of the best, absolute best,
investments you can make in your health.

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:13.751
Because then you can talk the talk with professionals,
and they take you more seriously, they give

00:07:13.751 --> 00:07:16.180
you better advice, they give you the straight
scoop.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.620
So this is all a very good investment of time.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:21.669
You can find a glossary of these terms and
more in the show notes for this episode at

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:23.210
tim.blog.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:24.669
And I do want to mention one other thing.

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:29.440
If you want an incredible rotating view of
different muscles like the QL—we talk about

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.460
the quadratus lumborum; that’s sort of this
squarish, rectangular muscle in the lower-back

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:42.250
area that is sort of the Grand Central Station
of all sorts of things—if you want to see

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:48.630
anything—the psoas major—check out the
Essential Anatomy 5 app for iOS and Android,

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.550
and you can see all of this.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.050
You can also see the circulatory system and
all sorts of other things.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:54.470
It’s a great app.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:55.479
Really enjoyed it.

00:07:55.479 --> 00:08:00.379
And there are short YouTube tutorials that
I recommend taking a look at if you end up

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:01.909
downloading it.

00:08:01.909 --> 00:08:06.410
And that was referred to me by professional
drummer Dave Elitch, who helps people improve

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:09.910
their mechanics, technique, and much more.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:11.460
And there you have it.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:16.919
So if you want a video to go with this, I
did record video of this conversation, and

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:20.229
I get up and walk around, and she does an
assessment with me live.

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:25.199
You can go to my YouTube channel, YouTube.com/TimFerriss—two
r’s, two s’s—and that will have some

00:08:25.199 --> 00:08:28.069
helpful graphics and so on overlaid into the
video.

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:32.700
OK that’s quite a bit, guys, but I think
it is a helpful prelude.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.650
And now, without further ado, please enjoy
this wide-ranging conversation with Dr. Shirley

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:39.680
Sahrmann.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:41.750
***
Tim Ferriss: Dr. Sahrmann, Shirley, welcome

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:42.750
to the show.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:48.620
It's so nice to have you with me today, and
I can't wait to ask a whole host of different

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.380
questions, so thank you for making the time.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.820
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: My pleasure.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:57.839
Tim Ferriss: I'd like to begin, perhaps, with
the connective tissue that led to you being

00:08:57.839 --> 00:09:04.959
on the show today, which is a friend of mine
and a well-known, I suppose, the label "performance

00:09:04.959 --> 00:09:08.900
coach" could be applied, he also has a background
in physical therapy, Eric Cressey.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:15.940
He works with many major league baseball players,
has a high degree of success with pitchers

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.029
specifically, but he has written.

00:09:18.029 --> 00:09:25.140
He also mentioned to me that "Diagnosis and
Treatment of Movement Impairment Syndromes

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.350
is probably the book…"

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.769
and I'm quoting him here, "...is probably
the book that has influenced me more than

00:09:28.769 --> 00:09:30.260
any other in my career.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.540
It's worth every penny."

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:38.800
I'm curious why it is that this book seems
to have been so revolutionary for him and

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.410
many others.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:44.850
What would you say explains that or differentiates
that book?

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:49.980
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, I think the one
big objective in it — I've actually been

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:55.550
a physical therapist for over 60 years.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:10:03.690
During all of that time I've been through
different eras of changes in physical therapy.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:11.240
Where I've sort of gotten to is how movement
basically induces pathology.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:17.100
Part of that, trying to explain that and how
it works, is also developing diagnostic categories

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.120
that direct physical therapy treatment.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:27.420
What this book was about was a first attempt
to really put together diagnostic categories

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:34.550
that are based on movement and movement as
an inducer of musculoskeletal problems.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:41.480
Also, kind of working on the background of
what are the tissue adaptations that contribute

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.769
to this?

00:10:43.769 --> 00:10:51.700
It really was an organizational attempt to
identify for — the first book covered the

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:57.480
back and the shoulder and the hip, so I guess
the shoulder is one of the things that he

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.050
must have been particularly interested in
if he's dealing with pitchers.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:05.820
The shoulder's really quite complex because
you've got that shoulder blade, as well as

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:12.959
the glenohumeral joint, and it's not as easy
as muscles just turn on or turn off appropriately.

00:11:12.959 --> 00:11:15.630
They've got to really be well coordinated.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:23.290
I think that putting together this kind of
information in a way that could be understood

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:29.290
by a whole variety of people — in fact,
I was so slow in getting it out that I was

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:34.750
grateful that there was the internet and Amazon
selling things, because if it would've only

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:40.980
been sold in medical bookstores, no one like
Eric would've ever found it.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:46.180
That was one of the advantages of being a
slow writer.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:51.779
Of course I learned more while all of that
was happening, too.

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:59.040
Tim Ferriss: How did that attempt, or maybe
not attempt, how did that organizational approach

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:00.280
—
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: No, it was an attempt.

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:05.300
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, and also the maybe reframing
of movement in the way that you just described,

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:09.770
differ from what came before or what was predominant
at the time?

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:16.370
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, to be perfectly
honest with you, Tim, it's not like this insight

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:22.350
has been taken over by even the large majority
of the people in my profession.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:27.760
It's still a bit of a struggle to have people
move in this direction for a whole variety

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:29.910
of reasons.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.610
Typically, even though I wasn't there when
physical therapy was first started, I wasn't

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:36.820
too far behind.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:43.300
Typically, the role of the physical therapist
was the doctor figured out what the problem

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:50.760
was, made the diagnosis, and the physical
therapist really provided treatment for what

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:55.140
I think could fairly be called the symptoms
or the consequences of that problem.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:59.630
In fact, I am old enough that I actually saw
polio patients.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:06.250
The vaccine had just come out about when I
was entering physical therapy school, so we

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:15.230
had a role in providing the therapy for the
doctor's identified condition.

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:19.430
That's very different than what I'm proposing,
or have proposed with this book.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:23.060
I think what's beginning to be recognized.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:28.750
I think the other thing that's so important
about all this, I'm sure you are a reflection

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:34.440
of this, is in the old days, no one thought
lifestyle had anything to do with your health.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:35.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:38.720
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I always like to point
out this story.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:43.300
My family cooked with so much Crisco I don't
know how my blood flows.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:51.830
If the green beans were too healthy, we had
bacon grease to put on them.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:52.830
I was very fortunate.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:58.110
I worked with a physician for a while who
was really leading the way in showing about

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:04.540
the role of exercise and nutrition in — he
did what that's really called translational

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:09.630
research, showing the cellular changes in
animals, and then also running studies in

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:11.070
older people.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:17.660
It was like an amazing insight for me to realize
that your lifestyle had something to do with

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:18.660
it.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:25.170
I think that's behind what's slowly emerging
as seeing movement play a different role.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:29.990
I think what I'd like to get across to people,
it's not inevitable what's going to happen

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:37.680
to you, that you can do things via lifestyle
to improve what your outcome's going to be.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.800
Tim Ferriss: I would love to come back to,
I believe, and I don't want to misquote you,

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:46.990
but something you said which is, "The treatment
of symptoms.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:51.710
So many offices are treating symptoms, perhaps
not root causes."

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:52.710
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:57.620
Tim Ferriss: I have read, and you can't believe
everything you read on the internet, so please

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:00.519
correct me if I that's wrong, but that you've
—

00:15:00.519 --> 00:15:01.519
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Is that a new saying?

00:15:01.519 --> 00:15:02.519
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:15:02.519 --> 00:15:03.519
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Wow.

00:15:03.519 --> 00:15:08.130
Tim Ferriss: That you've described low back
pain as not a diagnosis, but a symptom.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.450
Could you just speak to that?

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:16.130
Because as someone who currently for the last
maybe six to nine months has had a very perplexing

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:24.390
constellation of symptoms that I describe
as low back pain, this I think will resonate

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.959
with many people who are listening.

00:15:26.959 --> 00:15:30.750
Would you mind elaborating on low back pain
as a symptom and not a diagnosis?

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:34.020
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, I mean, just what
you're saying, you're saying it's low back

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:35.020
pain.

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:38.330
You're just telling me that you've got pain
and you're telling me where you've got pain.

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:39.490
That is clearly a symptom.

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:40.769
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, right.

00:15:40.769 --> 00:15:48.100
I am from Long Island, so sometimes I ask
the silliest of questions, but got to start

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:49.100
with the basics.

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:50.100
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:51.100
There you go.

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:54.860
Well, the nice part is you can actually get
reimbursed for making that big, clever diagnosis.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:59.240
Even without an MD degree.

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:01.130
No.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:04.390
Where I would be looking at that problem,
and I have an idea of what your problem is

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:05.410
—
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:06.410
Okay.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:07.410
Already.

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.730
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, just because — we
can talk about that.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:11.750
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:18.579
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I don't want to sound
too glib about it, but so what I would be

00:16:18.579 --> 00:16:27.370
doing is naming your low back pain by the
movement that most consistently causes your

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:34.790
symptoms, by changing that movement, reduces
or eliminates your symptoms.

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:38.920
Then I'm talking to you about a real cause
of the problem.

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:43.709
Now, it's not going down to the tissue level
and saying, "Well, it's a disc or a facet

00:16:43.709 --> 00:16:46.040
joint," or any of the rest of it.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:53.019
Here, again, in some ways when you have a
problem like that, you can't say in the back

00:16:53.019 --> 00:16:57.199
that one tissue's at fault, because a lot
of tissues have to change if you're having

00:16:57.199 --> 00:17:02.269
pain coming from your back region.

00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:08.260
The expertise of a physical therapist needs
to be, what is the movement that's either

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:11.930
causing or exacerbating that problem?

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:17.130
Tim Ferriss: I'm curious to — well, maybe
we can dive into, you said that you thought

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:18.500
you might know what my issue is.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:19.809
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:17:19.809 --> 00:17:21.760
That's because I know you're a big exerciser.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:22.760
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:23.760
Yes, indeed.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.929
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I mean, do you want
me to just give you a ballpark idea?

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:27.520
Tim Ferriss: I do, absolutely.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:28.990
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:33.320
Well, because would you believe that abdominals
can get to be too much overdeveloped?

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:39.620
Tim Ferriss: It makes some intuitive sense,
but it's not something you hear many people

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:40.620
talk about.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:41.620
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I know, I know, I know.

00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:48.620
Even within the community of physical therapists,
people are really exercising big time.

00:17:48.620 --> 00:17:51.440
I mean, high-intensity exercise is super popular.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:57.190
I'm all for it because it'll increase our
patient load.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:18:01.740
One of the things that happens when your abdominals
are overdeveloped, because what happens when

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:08.320
muscles hypertrophy, they become stiffer,
and muscles are like springs.

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:11.550
They have — I mean, I'm using the mechanical
word of stiffness.

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:19.070
When the abdominals get to be too much, they
increase the compression on your spine.

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:24.140
The way you can check me out on this is if
you look to see if you take a deep breath,

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:29.060
if you go from maximum exhalation to maximum
inhalation, you should be able to change the

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:34.059
circumference of your rib cage about two and
a half to three inches.

00:18:34.059 --> 00:18:38.510
If you can't really do that, then it means
that the stiffness of your abdominal muscles

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:41.040
is so much it's adding to the compression.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:47.960
Then, if you have any kind of asymmetry, for
example, if you put your hands on your iliac

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:53.840
crest, and one iliac crest is slightly higher
than the other, then basically your spine

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:55.980
is in a side bend.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:59.890
If it's in a side bend, and you're squeezing
on your vertebrae, they're not happy because

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:03.700
they aren't lined up as optimally as possible.

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:04.700
Do you see what I mean?

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:05.700
Tim Ferriss: I do, absolutely.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:06.700
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.380
That's the ballpark idea.

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:12.510
That also reflects how we're looking at these
problems.

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.710
What is it about the way you move?

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:20.539
What is it about the way you've exercised
or done things that cause the symptoms?

00:19:20.539 --> 00:19:26.190
Tim Ferriss: I would love to spend more time
on this, selfishly of course, because the

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:32.170
reason I am sitting and not standing for this
interview is because of this lower back pain,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:33.820
which is actually —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So it's worse when you're

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:34.970
standing than when you're sitting?

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:39.640
Tim Ferriss: It is worse when I am standing.

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:45.190
Now, I do have — I guess what — we can
jump right into the weeds.

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:50.820
I have a transitional segment, if I'm using
the right terminology, in my lumbar, so I

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:55.910
do have quite a bit of excessive lower back
sway or atypical lower back sway, just for

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:56.910
people listening.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:59.650
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Now, when you say sway,
do you mean an increased curve?

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:06.559
Tim Ferriss: Increased curve, yeah, like lordosis
and kind of guts hanging out, and with that

00:20:06.559 --> 00:20:10.630
anterior pelvic tilt, right?

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:16.920
Standing and slow walking, say walking through
a museum, tend to aggravate it the most.

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:22.960
My brother has the same thing, although in
the last six months or so when I sit on a

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:29.780
very hard surface, like a hard bench or something
like that, it also causes this pain.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:34.450
I have had imaging, but maybe we could talk
about imaging, how you see some people who

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:38.120
look like they've gone through a mulcher on
their back MRI, but they're asymptomatic,

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:39.120
and then you have the other.

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:40.120
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:41.120
Right.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:42.120
That's the whole point.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:43.120
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:44.120
Then you have the opposite.

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:49.540
I do have some stenosis around L4, L5, but
the pain feels to be localized around the

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:51.020
SI joint.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:55.410
The relief, if this is helpful, I know we're
getting a little technical for some folks,

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:59.270
but the relief that I've had in the last week
was actually from seeing a chiropractor.

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:03.130
There's a high degree of variability with
chiropractors, but he works with a lot of

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:04.130
athletes.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:07.100
He put me on a machine that provided some
traction.

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:08.100
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: There's traction.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:09.100
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:12.210
He said, "I think it's actually that you may
have a disc pressing on a nerve that runs

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:18.980
past the SI joint, so you're misattributing
the cause to the SI."

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:25.260
I've had quite a bit of relief, but to answer
your question, standing, slow walking combined

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:29.990
with standing, like going through a museum
or a cocktail party, sitting on hard surfaces,

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:32.100
those are the three things that hurt.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.750
Brisk walking does not hurt.

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:40.090
Actually that type of, this is a primitive
interpretation, but sort of repeated stretching

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:45.200
of the hip flexors, if I'm getting enough
terminal hip extension, feels really good

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:46.200
to the back.

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:47.200
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yep.

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:48.200
Yep.

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:49.200
Tim Ferriss: Those are a few of the things.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:52.710
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Have you put your hands
on your pelvis to see when you're walking

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:53.710
if it rotates?

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.230
Tim Ferriss: I have not.

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:58.220
I would love to know how to do that properly.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:01.720
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, I mean, it's not
rocket science.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.360
You know where your pelvis, you know where
your hands are.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:05.409
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:22:05.409 --> 00:22:11.570
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Just because very often
when your hip flexors are not even just not

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:16.690
short, just stiff, stiffer than your back
as you walk, it rotates your pelvis.

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:19.179
That's where you're going to be getting your
symptoms from.

00:22:19.179 --> 00:22:24.279
Evidently when you go fast enough, you're
not staying static and you're causing enough

00:22:24.279 --> 00:22:26.380
equal movement.

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:27.910
That would be the big thing.

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:31.950
When you stand up and you're in this anterior
tilt, can you contract your abdominals enough

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:33.090
to get out of the tilt?

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:34.130
Tim Ferriss: I can, yeah.

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:35.130
It seems as though I can.

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:36.400
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Does that decrease your
symptoms?

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:38.140
Tim Ferriss: It does decrease my symptoms.

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:39.140
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right.

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:44.020
Tim Ferriss: If my back is bothering me, I'll
very often do basically a forward fold or

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:47.440
a full squat and then round my back and get
into that flexed position.

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:48.640
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:53.919
Tim Ferriss: The flexed position and even
mild extension does not bother the back.

00:22:53.919 --> 00:22:59.400
If I do a compression test, like a heel drop
test or I pull myself into a chair, it's standing

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:05.510
straight up that, and with compression, that
shows that type of intolerance.

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:07.980
I get that pain kind of directly on the lower
spine.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:13.539
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: The other thing to try,
Tim, is when you stand up, put your feet apart.

00:23:13.539 --> 00:23:17.040
Separate them out and see if that changes
your symptoms.

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:18.040
Tim Ferriss: And what is that doing?

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:20.830
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, number one is
this little thing I referred to before.

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:25.920
If one iliac crest is higher than another,
and it's a test for what we call relative

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:28.080
stiffness.

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:33.260
If you are — one of the big hip flexor that's
problematic is called the tensor fasciae latae.

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:34.650
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:37.650
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: It's an abductor.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:42.490
If you put your feet apart, so your hips are
abducted, it takes the stretch off of that

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:44.190
band.

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:48.580
Any kind of asymmetry that you would have,
particularly with the transition vertebrae,

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:50.049
would be playing into the symptoms.

00:23:50.049 --> 00:23:51.169
Do you see what I mean?

00:23:51.169 --> 00:23:52.169
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:23:52.169 --> 00:23:53.471
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: You could find out,
yeah, and then if you put them together and

00:23:53.471 --> 00:23:58.799
your symptoms increased, then you would know
that that's what's playing a role, doing this.

00:23:58.799 --> 00:23:59.799
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:23:59.799 --> 00:24:04.450
I'll add a few more things just because this
is a rare opportunity to get to talk with

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:05.679
you about this.

00:24:05.679 --> 00:24:10.710
My TFL is very — tends to be very tight
and sensitive.

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:11.710
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That’s right.

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:12.710
Yeah.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:14.570
Tim Ferriss: The piriformis muscle is also
very tight.

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:17.350
A piece of this that may be helpful as far
as, may or may not be helpful, but what gives

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:24.490
my back also some release is working on the,
very specifically, the iliacus and then some

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:26.830
of the adductors on the inside of the thigh.

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:27.830
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:28.830
[inaudible].

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:29.830
Tell me what that means.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:34.659
Tim Ferriss: Well, having someone really dig
into the abdomen to have me, say, extend the

00:24:34.659 --> 00:24:35.659
leg almost.

00:24:35.659 --> 00:24:36.659
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

00:24:36.659 --> 00:24:37.659
It's not you working on it.

00:24:37.659 --> 00:24:38.659
Somebody else is working on it.

00:24:38.659 --> 00:24:39.659
Tim Ferriss: No, it's somebody else working
on it.

00:24:39.659 --> 00:24:41.940
Then, it's not very pleasant for people who
are listening.

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:48.110
Then some of my adductors, I don't know if
it's magnus, longus, or whatever, but also

00:24:48.110 --> 00:24:51.370
very tight and seemingly potentially weak.

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:58.809
To come back to the height, maybe the asymmetry
of the iliac crest, my right side seems to

00:24:58.809 --> 00:24:59.809
get hiked up a lot.

00:24:59.809 --> 00:25:00.809
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:25:00.809 --> 00:25:06.130
Tim Ferriss: Doing wall sits to try to press
them maybe back into some symmetry seems to

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:07.860
alleviate some of the symptoms as well.

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:10.169
I don't know if any of this makes any sense,
but —

00:25:10.169 --> 00:25:11.169
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: See, that's what I mean.

00:25:11.169 --> 00:25:15.070
You've just confirmed — one of my thoughts
is that if your right iliac crest is higher

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:20.690
than your left — but then I would also bet
that your right TFL is stiffer than your left.

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:24.980
If that's playing a role, then when you put
your feet apart, your iliac crest should level

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:27.280
out and that should help with your symptoms.

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:28.280
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:32.659
Historically when I've been recording podcasts,
I basically end up in that really wide stance.

00:25:32.659 --> 00:25:37.190
I think I'm — now, is that — I mean, that's
useful for maybe temporarily relieving the

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:39.210
symptoms if I'm recording a podcast.

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:41.169
In terms of corrective measures, let's just
say using your —

00:25:41.169 --> 00:25:44.540
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Have you ever tried
anything where you're in the quadruped position?

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:46.320
Tim Ferriss: I have, actually.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:50.289
A long time ago I did a lot of movement in
quadruped position, but I would be curious

00:25:50.289 --> 00:25:52.330
to hear what you have in mind.

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:56.230
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, because part of
what happens when one iliac crest stays higher

00:25:56.230 --> 00:26:00.559
than another, and I'm not — to be perfectly
honest with you, I haven't quite figured it

00:26:00.559 --> 00:26:06.299
all out yet, but there's some adaptation of
the other hip muscles.

00:26:06.299 --> 00:26:13.730
I've just found that if you do this in quadruped,
you just rock back, often it will improve

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:15.620
the asymmetry that you have.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:17.160
Tim Ferriss: Basically being on hands and
knees?

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:19.679
So, your knees —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Hands and knees, right.

00:26:19.679 --> 00:26:22.520
Let your hips drop to about 90 degrees.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:23.650
You don't have to go back all the way.

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:26.000
You just need to go back a little bit.

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:30.820
Go back by easily pushing with your hands,
because otherwise if you activate your hip

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:34.840
flexors, it could pull it, contribute to your
problems.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:35.840
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:36.840
Okay.

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.559
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Then, can you tolerate
prone face down?

00:26:38.559 --> 00:26:39.559
Tim Ferriss: I can, yeah.

00:26:39.559 --> 00:26:40.559
Yeah, I can tolerate prone.

00:26:40.559 --> 00:26:44.350
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And then you need to
just do — just flex your knee, and then

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:46.419
you need to laterally rotate your hips.

00:26:46.419 --> 00:26:52.070
You're letting your knee flex to 90 degrees
and then let your foot go in towards the other

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:53.070
leg.

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.070
That's lateral rotation.

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:55.070
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:59.230
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That kind of motion
will help to elongate the TFL.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:00.230
ITB.

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:01.350
Tim Ferriss: Interesting.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:03.190
And you're doing that leg by leg?

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:04.190
The [inaudible].

00:27:04.190 --> 00:27:05.190
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Oh, yeah.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:06.190
One leg at a time.

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:07.190
Tim Ferriss: One leg at a time.

00:27:07.190 --> 00:27:08.190
Okay.

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:09.190
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Do everything bilaterally,
yeah.

00:27:09.190 --> 00:27:10.640
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:11.640
Very interesting.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:12.640
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: You can try those things.

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:13.640
Let me know.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:14.640
Tim Ferriss: Great.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:15.640
I will.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:16.640
I will do both of those.

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:19.200
If we zoom out just for a moment — thank
you for that, by the way.

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:20.380
We may come back to it.

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:25.399
How would you describe the movement systems
syndromes approach?

00:27:25.399 --> 00:27:27.240
So, the MSS approach.

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:30.270
What would the sort of lay description of
that be?

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:33.961
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: It basically is putting
a formal name — in 2013, the American Physical

00:27:33.961 --> 00:27:38.240
Therapy Association adopted the movement system
as its identity.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:44.279
To me what's really important about all of
this is that it's a way of trying to say to

00:27:44.279 --> 00:27:51.039
the public that there is a body system called
the movement system.

00:27:51.039 --> 00:27:59.230
It's not like the traditional anatomically
defined systems, like the cardiovascular system

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:02.940
or the musculoskeletal system or the nervous
system.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:10.130
It's a system of systems, but that's just
like, in my mind, the immune system, which

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:13.840
nothing is more important in medicine these
days than the immune system.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:14.950
It's a system of systems.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:19.570
It uses many of the different organs in its
function.

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:21.309
Metabolic system is the same way.

00:28:21.309 --> 00:28:25.730
When you think of it as running from subcellular
all the way up to how do you move in your

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:28.080
environment, movement is critical.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.080
The movement stops, everything stops.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:31.080
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:35.250
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I think we take — in
some ways, to me, it's like a parallel to

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:43.289
the nutrition system because we take for granted
doing it.

00:28:43.289 --> 00:28:46.880
Yet, there's right ways and there are wrong
ways.

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:52.679
The whole idea of this is to realize that
their movement does involve a system.

00:28:52.679 --> 00:28:58.330
Just like we were talking about before, movement
— if you have a lesion in a system, like

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:03.440
you have rheumatoid arthritis or something,
or you have a stroke, then you've got pathology

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:04.980
in your movement.

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:09.480
As I indicated, movement can also induce pathology.

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:15.210
In fact, we know if people don't move enough,
they develop the metabolic system syndrome

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:19.990
and other kinds of things because for lack
of movement.

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:26.880
How important it is to move, this is related
to the lifestyle issue and doing it right.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:30.830
One of the things I always loved doing with
patients was saying, "So, who taught you to

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:31.830
walk?"

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:32.830
They say "Nobody."

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:34.150
I say, "That's the problem."

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:37.250
Just because you're doing it doesn't mean
you're doing it right.

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:38.250
You're just doing it.

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:39.250
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:40.250
Yeah.

00:29:40.250 --> 00:29:43.210
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Just like you, if you're
walking and you're getting lumbopelvic rotation,

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:44.550
that's playing into your problem.

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:46.260
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:52.179
Also, if I don't have, which I don't think
I do — this is one pattern of diagnosis

00:29:52.179 --> 00:29:58.269
with me that I think is accurate, that I don't
have much terminal hip extension, so when

00:29:58.269 --> 00:30:01.870
I walk, I'm using my lower back to fake hip
extension.

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:03.460
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That's what I'm telling
you.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:04.460
Exactly.

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:05.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:06.460
Okay.

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:07.460
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: See, that's what I'm
saying.

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:08.460
You really can't do that.

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:09.520
I mean, there's no way you won't keep injuring
your back if you keep walking like that.

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:10.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:19.049
How do you re-pattern or help people to adopt
so that they can use it subconsciously, new

00:30:19.049 --> 00:30:21.039
motor patterns or new movement patterns?

00:30:21.039 --> 00:30:23.029
Because I've been doing this for God knows
how many decades.

00:30:23.029 --> 00:30:24.740
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, not that many.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:26.130
You don't look that old.

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:27.710
Tim Ferriss: Thank you.

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:28.710
Thank you.

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:29.710
I appreciate that.

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:31.280
My new best friend.

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:36.150
How do you help patients to get to that point
where they've changed something as fundamental

00:30:36.150 --> 00:30:37.210
as how they walk?

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:39.950
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, in my mind it's
twofold.

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:42.960
In one, we know it takes a while.

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:47.900
Just like if you're learning a sport or you're
learning to do anything, it takes time and

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:52.200
it takes attention and it takes specificity.

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:57.210
One is, just like we're talking about with
you, what are the most important kinds of

00:30:57.210 --> 00:30:58.210
exercises?

00:30:58.210 --> 00:31:02.990
What are the issues that are an impediment
to doing it the way you should do it?

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:07.480
What are specific exercises that can help
you and minimize those?

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:13.780
Then, what's really important is showing you
in your everyday activities what you should

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:14.780
do.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:20.940
For example, if you already know that your
hip flexors, you're calling it the iliacus,

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:27.130
is problematic then even when you're sitting,
making sure you're not pulling with your hip

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:28.179
flexors to stay forward when you're sitting.

00:31:28.179 --> 00:31:29.350
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm probably doing that
right now.

00:31:29.350 --> 00:31:30.570
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I know.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.029
I've been watching you.

00:31:33.029 --> 00:31:36.799
It's how you even roll over or get out of
bed.

00:31:36.799 --> 00:31:42.250
We go through every one of these things teaching
you in your everyday activities so that it

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:49.110
does become automatic, but it obviously takes
participation on the patient's or the subject's

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:52.809
part as well to learn it.

00:31:52.809 --> 00:31:56.190
Then, it's going to take time.

00:31:56.190 --> 00:32:00.659
I think it's important for people to realize
they can't just do 10 repetitions or three

00:32:00.659 --> 00:32:03.600
sets of an exercise and then they're going
to move differently.

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:10.890
I think that's been part of what's been picked
up in my book is that you have to bring people

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:15.679
along, show them how to do that, and that
exercise won't change the way you move.

00:32:15.679 --> 00:32:19.010
You have to change the way you move, and that
can improve how muscles function.

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:21.250
Tim Ferriss: You mentioned the psoas.

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:23.020
I'd actually like to come back to the psoas.

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:27.800
In my particular case, because I have been
an aggressive athlete, or was for several

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:32.999
decades and accrued an impressive number of
fractures and surgeries and so on, I get manual

00:32:32.999 --> 00:32:33.999
—
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Oh, really?

00:32:33.999 --> 00:32:34.999
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah.

00:32:34.999 --> 00:32:38.789
So I get manual therapy once a week, some
type of soft tissue treatment.

00:32:38.789 --> 00:32:44.700
And what I found for me personally is it seems
like psoas major and so on, when someone does

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:51.710
a manual release, for whatever reason, when
they get sort of inside that pelvis a bit

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:56.620
more to what I've been told is the iliac,
is that's when I feel the most symptomatic

00:32:56.620 --> 00:32:58.850
relief for my back.

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:01.220
But the psoas seems to hold some importance.

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:07.700
I don't know if you could speak to that, but
in terms of the role of psoas overactivity

00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:14.679
as it relates to back pain, is that something
that you still feel is something people pay

00:33:14.679 --> 00:33:15.720
too little attention to?

00:33:15.720 --> 00:33:16.720
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Oh, yeah.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:20.309
I mean, I don't think it's always the cause,
but it can certainly be an exacerbator, because

00:33:20.309 --> 00:33:25.860
the psoas attaches to the lumbar vertebrae,
and it also attaches to the intervertebral

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:27.110
disc.

00:33:27.110 --> 00:33:32.669
So it's a muscle that's constantly pulling
on your back and pulling it in sort of a translation

00:33:32.669 --> 00:33:34.360
motion.

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:39.520
Iliac is attached to your pelvis, so it is
not directly acting on the vertebrae the same

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:41.030
way the psoas is.

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:48.659
So in fact, people that truly have a herniated
disc and they're in that acute phase, I try

00:33:48.659 --> 00:33:51.730
to have them do nothing with their iliopsoas.

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:56.980
If they want to lift their leg up, use your
hand to lift your leg up to put your shoes

00:33:56.980 --> 00:34:02.889
on or to cross your legs or get into the car
or something if you're sitting down already,

00:34:02.889 --> 00:34:05.509
so you minimize that use.

00:34:05.509 --> 00:34:10.149
And just like we were talking about in quadruped,
if you are in quadruped and you want to rock

00:34:10.149 --> 00:34:15.359
back, you will probably use the psoas to do
it.

00:34:15.359 --> 00:34:19.149
And that's why I suggested to you to push
with your hands so you go back and you don't

00:34:19.149 --> 00:34:20.149
use that muscle.

00:34:20.149 --> 00:34:25.100
Well, these are small samples of what I'm
talking about as far as identifying what are

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:28.870
the factors that are contributing and how
do you change that in your everyday activities.

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:29.870
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:34:29.870 --> 00:34:34.670
What would be some other repeating culprits
that you see?

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:39.290
Let's just say someone has the symptom of
low back pain.

00:34:39.290 --> 00:34:44.540
You take them through an assessment or identify
that they have an overactive psoas.

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:51.550
What might be some other low-hanging fruit
with respect to helping them to identify common

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:56.020
patterns or positions that contribute to that
overactivity?

00:34:56.020 --> 00:35:01.680
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, in some ways I
would be looking more specifically at which

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:06.170
particular movements do it and then try to
identify.

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:15.540
For example, it would be hard for me to believe
this, but if your hip flexors — and it makes

00:35:15.540 --> 00:35:16.599
a difference.

00:35:16.599 --> 00:35:21.570
The tensor fascia latae is a hip flexor, but
it has a rotational component that's much

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:23.770
stronger than the psoas does.

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:26.410
The psoas has more anterior pull.

00:35:26.410 --> 00:35:31.050
The tensor fascia latae is going to pull more
on the pelvis.

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:37.090
And I will tell you that in my judgment, and
I don't think I'm way off on this, at least

00:35:37.090 --> 00:35:41.000
70 percent of the people with back pain, it's
because their hip's not moving optimally.

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:46.510
And you said it yourself: "My hip is not moving
and then it bothers my back."

00:35:46.510 --> 00:35:48.200
Well, that's exactly what goes on.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:54.380
And it doesn't take some big structural fault
to have that, just a difference in the passive

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:55.380
tension.

00:35:55.380 --> 00:36:02.290
So usually with younger people, if I'm going
to generalize about back pain, it's related

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:04.940
to their spine flexing.

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:09.390
Because when you're younger, you're more flexible.

00:36:09.390 --> 00:36:13.089
And I think the other thing that's tied to
that, while we're talking about the hip, is

00:36:13.089 --> 00:36:18.940
they're identifying more and more that hips,
what they call femoroacetabular impingement,

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:21.420
hips aren't flexing as much.

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:23.599
There's structural changes going on.

00:36:23.599 --> 00:36:27.140
So if your hip only flexes 90 degrees, then
you want to bend over, you're going to do

00:36:27.140 --> 00:36:31.240
it much more in your back because your hip's
not doing it.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:35.000
So I want to know all the things that relate
to flexion.

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:41.510
In the older person, then it's more related
to extension, just like the stenosis thing.

00:36:41.510 --> 00:36:45.030
Stenosis is, that's when you really can't
extend.

00:36:45.030 --> 00:36:49.900
That's why you see old people walking bent
over, why they need a walker when they're

00:36:49.900 --> 00:36:51.730
bending over.

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:54.380
And then the element of rotation.

00:36:54.380 --> 00:37:00.570
And because it's not just, is it one iliopsoas
that's problematic, or both of them, one tensor

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:05.000
that's pulling more strongly than the other
— and that's the passive tension, not just

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:06.180
the active tension.

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:07.579
And that's what you have to know.

00:37:07.579 --> 00:37:13.010
Tim Ferriss: Now, you were asking about my
breathing and the deep breath.

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:19.180
When I met with Eric, and I could be totally
screwing up this terminology, so you may need

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:23.170
to rein me in, but had me take off my shirt.

00:37:23.170 --> 00:37:29.380
And I think it was, I had a very low, it might
be high, but infrasternal angle.

00:37:29.380 --> 00:37:31.960
So I have a bit of a depression in the chest.

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:37.589
I have a very minimal ability to expand my
rib cage.

00:37:37.589 --> 00:37:39.040
I'm a belly breather.

00:37:39.040 --> 00:37:47.319
And I've had a number of people note that
it's likely when I breathe, I kind of rotate

00:37:47.319 --> 00:37:50.880
my entire rib cage backwards, which also causes
that excessive —

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:52.430
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: [inaudible].

00:37:52.430 --> 00:37:53.599
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:37:53.599 --> 00:37:56.690
That excessive hinging at the lumbar.

00:37:56.690 --> 00:38:01.530
What do you do with somebody who's got this
type of predicament or pattern?

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:06.550
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, usually, as I
was saying before, if your rib cage doesn't

00:38:06.550 --> 00:38:10.569
expand, then it's often because your abdominals
are too taut.

00:38:10.569 --> 00:38:11.569
Tim Ferriss: I see.

00:38:11.569 --> 00:38:17.040
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And so one of the ways
is to elongate them, so arms up over your

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:24.980
head and take in a deep breath, and just trying
to increase the — I mean, you probably know

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:28.000
that with breathing you've got two modes of
movement.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:31.359
One they call pump handle and the other is
bucket handle.

00:38:31.359 --> 00:38:33.440
Tim Ferriss: I'd love for you to elaborate
on that.

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:34.440
Yeah.

00:38:34.440 --> 00:38:35.440
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:39.869
Well, pump handle means the front of your
chest, your sternum, is going up like a pump,

00:38:39.869 --> 00:38:43.850
and bucket handle is like the sides expand.

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:48.510
And so with arms up overhead, you want to
really think about now lifting your chest

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:50.589
as well as pushing your rib cage out.

00:38:50.589 --> 00:38:51.609
Tim Ferriss: Laterally, you mean?

00:38:51.609 --> 00:38:52.609
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:38:52.609 --> 00:38:53.849
Laterally, like it's all going up.

00:38:53.849 --> 00:38:55.819
And then — yep, yep.

00:38:55.819 --> 00:38:56.819
Can you do that?

00:38:56.819 --> 00:38:59.480
Can you take —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's sad.

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:02.080
Yeah, I don't have much.

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:03.080
It doesn't do much.

00:39:03.080 --> 00:39:04.080
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, I know, but that
—

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:05.080
Tim Ferriss: As it stands right now, yeah.

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:06.650
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That's what you need
to do.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:11.670
And then here's the other thing that if you
stand with your back against the wall, and

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:17.920
then you try to do a side bend, but, but,
but, you want to make sure the side bend you're

00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:22.470
moving through, that the axis of rotation's
through your chest, not in your lumbar spine.

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:23.470
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:24.470
Yeah, that'll be —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Because that would make

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:25.470
—
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it'd make my symptoms worse.

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:29.450
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And you want to be sure,
Tim, that you don't just pull yourself over.

00:39:29.450 --> 00:39:33.220
You want to try to fall like you've got a
heavy elbow.

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:38.280
In other words, don't contract the muscles
on the same side, but try to get these to

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:40.310
elongate, so like a falling over —
Tim Ferriss: Right, on the opposite side.

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:45.349
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: — more passive elongation
rather than an active contraction.

00:39:45.349 --> 00:39:50.869
Now, you're not going to go real far initially,
unless you've got a really heavy elbow.

00:39:50.869 --> 00:39:54.829
Tim Ferriss: So the axis of rotation should
be —

00:39:54.829 --> 00:39:55.829
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Through the middle of
your chest.

00:39:55.829 --> 00:39:57.530
Tim Ferriss: — or the fulcrum should be
the middle of the chest.

00:39:57.530 --> 00:39:58.530
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, right.

00:39:58.530 --> 00:40:03.030
I always like to say, I don't care how far
you go, I care how you get there.

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:04.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, okay.

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:08.950
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Don't push it for big
range, and just make sure you're not moving

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:11.599
your lumbar spine and that you're doing your
thoracic spine.

00:40:11.599 --> 00:40:15.819
Tim Ferriss: And are you breathing in those
positions or is the stretching your intercostals?

00:40:15.819 --> 00:40:18.940
Or what is the objective of the side bend?

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:22.119
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: The objective is to
elongate those abdominals that are not letting

00:40:22.119 --> 00:40:23.119
you —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I see.

00:40:23.119 --> 00:40:26.860
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And the other thing
is just even when you were doing this — well,

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:29.359
you don't have any symptoms at all when you're
sitting there right now?

00:40:29.359 --> 00:40:32.560
Tim Ferriss: I have a little tightness in
my low back.

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:34.599
I'm sitting in a chair with lumbar support.

00:40:34.599 --> 00:40:38.190
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Why do you do that when
you have too much lumbar curve anyway?

00:40:38.190 --> 00:40:43.359
Tim Ferriss: Well, because I've noticed that
for whatever reason, symptomatically, I get

00:40:43.359 --> 00:40:46.550
relief with a small amount of lumbar support
—

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:47.550
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: With a small amount,
okay.

00:40:47.550 --> 00:40:49.850
Yeah, because you're —
Tim Ferriss: — because a flat back chair,

00:40:49.850 --> 00:40:55.730
and I end up kind of falling forward and flattening
my back, it ends up hurting me much more later.

00:40:55.730 --> 00:40:58.030
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, because you're
getting a translation motion.

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:01.599
But okay, what if you put your arms up over
your head and take a deep breath?

00:41:01.599 --> 00:41:04.349
Does that decrease your symptoms at all?

00:41:04.349 --> 00:41:10.790
Tim Ferriss: I would say doesn't noticeably
— I don't have any severe symptoms right

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:11.790
this instant.

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:12.790
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: No, but it doesn't,
okay.

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:14.069
Tim Ferriss: It doesn't worsen it, for sure.

00:41:14.069 --> 00:41:15.850
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But it doesn't make
it any better either.

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:23.630
Tim Ferriss: It might lessen it slightly.

00:41:23.630 --> 00:41:27.310
The degree of pain right now I would say is
pretty low, so it's hard for me to monitor.

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:31.140
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That's good and again,
if you look at it from the standpoint, the

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:36.130
more you move someplace else besides your
back, the better off you're going to be.

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:37.130
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, definitely.

00:41:37.130 --> 00:41:42.609
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I mean, so that's part
of the whole strategy is make sure, because

00:41:42.609 --> 00:41:48.000
usually the problem is that motion that's
problematic is occurring during all of your

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:49.350
activities.

00:41:49.350 --> 00:41:51.230
The body follows the rules of physics.

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:53.510
It takes the path of least resistance.

00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:57.900
So if it's easy to move there, it keeps moving
there, and that's what you're trying to change

00:41:57.900 --> 00:42:02.660
to make it easier to move at other places
where you should be moving more.

00:42:02.660 --> 00:42:05.400
Tim Ferriss: This seems to me to be very,
very, very important.

00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:11.849
Would you mind just saying that again, just
reiterating that the body follows the path

00:42:11.849 --> 00:42:16.540
of least resistance, so if you have a worn
groove and it's leading to pathology, you

00:42:16.540 --> 00:42:18.780
need to sort of grease a different groove?

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:20.130
Could you just speak to that?

00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:21.640
Because it strikes me —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: You're doing a beautiful

00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:22.640
job yourself.

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:23.640
I don't think you need me.

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:26.810
Tim Ferriss: Well, I'm great at pontificating.

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:28.490
I just need to change my movements.

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:31.089
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay, Father Superior.

00:42:31.089 --> 00:42:34.569
Tim Ferriss: No, I talk a good game.

00:42:34.569 --> 00:42:38.420
I just have to fix my movement patterns and
my breathing.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.140
But I like how you phrase it.

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:43.430
I mean, the body is going to take the path
of least resistance.

00:42:43.430 --> 00:42:44.790
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Exactly.

00:42:44.790 --> 00:42:48.390
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to follow these
exercises.

00:42:48.390 --> 00:42:51.130
I'll experiment with the elongation.

00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:58.819
I wanted to add one more data point, which
is if I do, for instance, Pilates classes

00:42:58.819 --> 00:43:02.360
with someone who's very technical, if I'm
in that flexed position, which tends to be

00:43:02.360 --> 00:43:07.840
more comfortable for my back, if I'm experiencing
a lot of symptoms —

00:43:07.840 --> 00:43:09.050
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Tell me, are you supine?

00:43:09.050 --> 00:43:10.310
Tim Ferriss: I am supine.

00:43:10.310 --> 00:43:11.310
Yes.

00:43:11.310 --> 00:43:12.310
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:13.310
You're supine.

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:14.310
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:15.310
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And then what are you
going to do?

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:21.000
Tim Ferriss: Basically what I'm trying to
sort out for myself is if the overly contracted

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:30.250
abdominal resting state can contribute to
the symptoms I'm experiencing, what I have

00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:36.100
also experienced is if I do a workout that
seems to be focused or is focused on a lot

00:43:36.100 --> 00:43:40.920
of core musculature and pelvis work and so
on, that my low back doesn't bother me for

00:43:40.920 --> 00:43:43.160
a few hours after that workout.

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:46.240
And I don't know how to interpret that.

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:47.740
Maybe these things are not at odds.

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:50.319
Maybe they can both be true for different
reasons.

00:43:50.319 --> 00:43:53.200
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, I mean, it depends
on what your workout is.

00:43:53.200 --> 00:43:56.960
But what would be bad in the long run is if
you're doing a lot of holding your legs up

00:43:56.960 --> 00:44:02.530
and moving those around while you're supine,
because again, you're going to be using your

00:44:02.530 --> 00:44:06.520
iliopsoas, and it's going to be pulling on
your back.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:09.490
The big thing is for you to be able to contract.

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:14.490
I'm getting the picture from you that I didn't
have before, that here you are with an increased

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:18.790
lumbar curve and that your lower abdominals
aren't as taut.

00:44:18.790 --> 00:44:23.410
And lower abdominals, I mean external obliques,
not just the ones that are lower.

00:44:23.410 --> 00:44:29.609
And when you contract those muscles that they
tend to flatten your back and decrease your

00:44:29.609 --> 00:44:30.609
symptoms.

00:44:30.609 --> 00:44:31.609
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:44:31.609 --> 00:44:32.609
Mm-hmm.

00:44:32.609 --> 00:44:36.670
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And so that would be
to me, what the advantage is when you're supine

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:38.859
and starting to do the exercise.

00:44:38.859 --> 00:44:46.260
What would be not the long run good is if
you're holding your legs up and trying to

00:44:46.260 --> 00:44:51.910
do something with your legs while you're holding
that position because you're building in too

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:55.390
much activity from the iliopsoas.

00:44:55.390 --> 00:44:59.410
So if anything, in that position, I would
just have you slide your leg down and try

00:44:59.410 --> 00:45:01.359
to keep your pelvis from tilting.

00:45:01.359 --> 00:45:07.390
Put your hands on your — I'm sure you probably
know what ASISes are: anterior superior iliac

00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:08.390
spine.

00:45:08.390 --> 00:45:09.390
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:45:09.390 --> 00:45:10.390
On the front of the of the hip.

00:45:10.390 --> 00:45:11.390
Yeah.

00:45:11.390 --> 00:45:12.390
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That's what I thought.

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:13.390
Yep, yep.

00:45:13.390 --> 00:45:14.390
And just make sure they don't tilt as you
slide —

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:15.390
Tim Ferriss: For people who are wondering,
could you just describe what that is since

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:16.970
—
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: It's a little bony prominence

00:45:16.970 --> 00:45:23.619
on the front of your pelvis, and some of your
major hip flexors attach there.

00:45:23.619 --> 00:45:29.150
But it's also an indication of what your pelvis
is doing as far as tilting forward or tilting

00:45:29.150 --> 00:45:30.730
backward.

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:34.329
And in your case, what you want to do is not
have it tilt forward.

00:45:34.329 --> 00:45:40.200
So the importance of that exercise would be
that you can move your legs without your pelvis

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:41.200
tilting anteriorly.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:42.200
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:44.640
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But I wouldn't put a
big load on them.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:50.830
I would only have them slide down like you're
sliding your heel along the supporting surface.

00:45:50.830 --> 00:45:53.349
Get it all the way down on one leg with no
tilt.

00:45:53.349 --> 00:45:55.280
See if you can do the same thing with the
other.

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:59.980
If you have difficulty, from what we've talked
about before, if you take them out in abduction,

00:45:59.980 --> 00:46:04.339
you should be able to get them down easier
because your tensor is going to be pulling

00:46:04.339 --> 00:46:05.590
on your pelvis as well.

00:46:05.590 --> 00:46:06.590
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:10.500
So for people listening, if I'm interpreting
this correctly, if your legs are separated,

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:15.300
so your legs end up more in a snow angel type
of position as opposed to directly in front

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:16.300
of your hips.

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:21.230
Now is that predominantly a diagnostic or
is it also a training move, that sliding heel?

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:22.230
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: All of the above.

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:23.390
Tim Ferriss: All the above.

00:46:23.390 --> 00:46:24.390
Okay.

00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:27.050
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I mean, that's the nice
thing about going through an exam in which

00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:33.069
you're looking for this path of least resistance,
the motion that is occurring too readily because

00:46:33.069 --> 00:46:36.420
it'll occur too readily with all of the activities.

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:39.150
It goes back to what we were saying before.

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:43.340
Then you try to make sure that you're either
not getting that motion or you're moving where

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:44.690
you should be moving.

00:46:44.690 --> 00:46:50.200
Yeah, so when you're taking people through
the exam, and I think this is what's so valuable,

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:54.140
is you're also showing them how to be in charge
of their symptoms because nothing is more

00:46:54.140 --> 00:46:57.950
scary than "Here comes the pain, what did
I do?

00:46:57.950 --> 00:46:58.950
How did I do it?

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:00.619
How do I get out of it?"

00:47:00.619 --> 00:47:05.080
And if you're showing people, if you go this
way, it hurts, if you do it this other way,

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:10.400
it doesn't hurt, and then — and that's helps
also with people following the program that's

00:47:10.400 --> 00:47:15.400
recommended because their symptoms are — they're
in charge of them, and they know what to do

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:16.400
to decrease them.

00:47:16.400 --> 00:47:23.369
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, this personal experience
has been incredibly frustrating, kind of horrifying,

00:47:23.369 --> 00:47:27.670
because it's the first time in my life — if
you tear a labrum in your shoulder or you

00:47:27.670 --> 00:47:35.079
break an arm or break a collarbone, it's oftentimes
reasonably straightforward, or it seems that

00:47:35.079 --> 00:47:36.079
way.

00:47:36.079 --> 00:47:40.560
Whereas, with this lower back pain, I would
feel better for three days, and then I would

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:45.369
wake up and I would just be in incredible,
like eight out of 10, nine out of 10 pain.

00:47:45.369 --> 00:47:48.839
And I could not identify what the cause was.

00:47:48.839 --> 00:47:54.569
And there've been times when my QL and my
external obliques and so on are so locked

00:47:54.569 --> 00:47:58.740
up in the paraspinals that I can stand for
a few minutes and I have to sit down, find

00:47:58.740 --> 00:47:59.869
something soft.

00:47:59.869 --> 00:48:01.829
And that's not the case right now.

00:48:01.829 --> 00:48:09.099
But the recurrence of symptoms has been so
unpredictable on some level that it's — and

00:48:09.099 --> 00:48:13.000
a friend of mine who's in the medical profession
said, "Oh, how long have you had that?"

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.319
And I said, "Nine months."

00:48:14.319 --> 00:48:18.530
And she said, "Well, you technically qualify
for someone with chronic pain."

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:21.490
And I was like, "Oh, my God, is this the new
normal?

00:48:21.490 --> 00:48:24.180
This cannot be the new normal."

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:29.119
Which is why I appreciate you taking so much
time speaking about this.

00:48:29.119 --> 00:48:35.560
When someone comes in and they have not identified
anything, they come to you for help or someone

00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:40.990
who's trained in your system, what does the
exam look like?

00:48:40.990 --> 00:48:43.110
What does the session look like?

00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:46.460
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, first, it's looking
at what they look like.

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:52.230
I mean, for me, just like if I saw you standing,
I would know a whole lot more than just looking

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:56.849
at you sitting because all of these things
that we've talked about indirectly, I would

00:48:56.849 --> 00:48:58.220
see immediately.

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:00.570
So one is just looking at alignment.

00:49:00.570 --> 00:49:05.390
And believe me, that's a roadmap to a whole
lot of things.

00:49:05.390 --> 00:49:10.829
And I think it's also worth noting that it's
why people stand the way they stand is to

00:49:10.829 --> 00:49:13.050
minimize energy expenditure.

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:18.430
So you can see what the passive tensions are,
which are reflective of how hypertrophied

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:19.890
the muscle is.

00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:22.410
And then it's simple motions, Tim.

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:24.490
Have people forward bend.

00:49:24.490 --> 00:49:26.020
Does their back flex too much?

00:49:26.020 --> 00:49:28.430
Does it not reverse its curve with you?

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:33.349
And how much do their hips flex?

00:49:33.349 --> 00:49:37.981
And typically, in men, there's more of a problem
of excessive lumbar flexion than there are

00:49:37.981 --> 00:49:43.160
in women just because the center of gravity
is higher, the hips are stiffer, et cetera.

00:49:43.160 --> 00:49:45.200
So how do they forward bend?

00:49:45.200 --> 00:49:46.880
How do they rotate?

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:51.390
Because many people will twist rather than
really be able to rotate.

00:49:51.390 --> 00:49:52.970
And then side bending.

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:54.330
Does it hurt?

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:55.330
Where do I see motion?

00:49:55.330 --> 00:49:59.190
Tim Ferriss: By twist, you mean their pelvis
follows them instead of that sort of —

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:00.190
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: No.

00:50:00.190 --> 00:50:01.190
Tim Ferriss: No?

00:50:01.190 --> 00:50:03.190
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: See, that's the other
thing that's interesting is because I'm sure

00:50:03.190 --> 00:50:07.049
with you, people have looked at what your
range of motion is in your hips.

00:50:07.049 --> 00:50:10.730
But when you're standing, you don't have that
same range of motion.

00:50:10.730 --> 00:50:14.540
Your pelvis won't rotate on your femurs the
same amount.

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:19.510
So one, does your hips not move and therefore
you have to move in your back —

00:50:19.510 --> 00:50:20.510
Tim Ferriss: The back.

00:50:20.510 --> 00:50:22.150
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: — because your pelvis
isn't rotating?

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:24.369
Or is it asymmetrical?

00:50:24.369 --> 00:50:27.869
And then the other thing is that actually,
ironically, if your abdominals are really

00:50:27.869 --> 00:50:33.210
good, instead of sort of rotating off more
of an axis, though it's not perfect, your

00:50:33.210 --> 00:50:39.151
trunk shifts over because if your trunk shifts
over in a twisting motion, it's because your

00:50:39.151 --> 00:50:40.800
abdominals won't elongate easily.

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:41.800
Tim Ferriss: Fascinating.

00:50:41.800 --> 00:50:46.760
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So we're looking for
— and then which one of these motions causes

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:47.760
symptoms?

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:49.660
When I see that it's bad, I will correct it.

00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:56.059
For example, if you had pain side-bending,
then I'd put my hand above your iliac crest,

00:50:56.059 --> 00:50:57.770
have you side-bend again.

00:50:57.770 --> 00:51:01.859
And if I've blocked it and you don't have
pain, then I know that that motion occurring

00:51:01.859 --> 00:51:03.200
there is causing your pain.

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:04.820
If when you —
Tim Ferriss: The movement of the pelvis —

00:51:04.820 --> 00:51:08.730
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: The movement of the
back —

00:51:08.730 --> 00:51:09.730
Tim Ferriss: — or the iliac crest.

00:51:09.730 --> 00:51:12.480
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And the same thing,
if you bend over and there's no pain, but

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:17.630
you come back up and you lead with your back
rather than your hips, then that causes pain.

00:51:17.630 --> 00:51:21.810
Then I know again, that extending is causing
your symptoms.

00:51:21.810 --> 00:51:26.510
And then if your symptoms, as you've reported,
get better when you bend over, again, I know

00:51:26.510 --> 00:51:29.640
that extension's causing your symptoms.

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:35.170
And then I go through little tests in supine
to see the length of the hip flexor muscles.

00:51:35.170 --> 00:51:39.200
I look to see what your symptoms are in supine.

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:44.349
If you have symptoms, just like you sort of
suggested, if we flex your hips and knees,

00:51:44.349 --> 00:51:48.559
you ought be more comfortable than when they're
down straight.

00:51:48.559 --> 00:51:52.270
If I abduct your hips, I know what's causing
it that way.

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:53.270
Do you see what I mean?

00:51:53.270 --> 00:51:54.270
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:51:54.270 --> 00:51:58.521
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So then I will passively
move your hips to see what the range is, make

00:51:58.521 --> 00:51:59.569
sure it's passive.

00:51:59.569 --> 00:52:04.260
Then I'll have you do it actively to see if
that elicits symptoms, show you how to change

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:05.260
it.

00:52:05.260 --> 00:52:10.680
I'm going through an exam looking for that
movement that shows up consistently.

00:52:10.680 --> 00:52:13.980
If I stop it or improve it, symptoms go down.

00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:23.660
So we do in the supine, side-lying, prone,
quadruped, sitting, watch people walk, everything

00:52:23.660 --> 00:52:24.770
that gives them pain.

00:52:24.770 --> 00:52:30.859
They get in and out of their car, how to roll,
how to go up and down stairs.

00:52:30.859 --> 00:52:33.301
Whatever activities would give them pain,
I'll go through them with them.

00:52:33.301 --> 00:52:39.000
Tim Ferriss: And is the assessment largely
the same for athletes versus non-athletes?

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:41.380
It's pretty much —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That's a really interesting

00:52:41.380 --> 00:52:49.309
point because in some ways people have a hard
time because I'm looking for little baby things.

00:52:49.309 --> 00:52:56.760
Ironically, there's really good research that's
been done by Linda Van Dillen, and these movements

00:52:56.760 --> 00:53:02.670
that cause the symptoms occur very early and
they're only a few degrees.

00:53:02.670 --> 00:53:06.700
And so sometimes with athletes I've had the
issue that, well, these are just baby things

00:53:06.700 --> 00:53:08.480
and they don't really matter.

00:53:08.480 --> 00:53:10.010
But they do matter.

00:53:10.010 --> 00:53:14.660
You've got to stop that, and then you can
build on bigger ways.

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:23.040
There's a therapist by the name of Robbie
Ohashi who's put these into — it's like

00:53:23.040 --> 00:53:25.450
a movement spectrum.

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:27.440
It's not the exact words.

00:53:27.440 --> 00:53:28.809
I'm blanking on it right now.

00:53:28.809 --> 00:53:36.069
Where you do isolated exercises, combined
exercises, and then more putting them together

00:53:36.069 --> 00:53:39.740
for your sport, whether it's tennis or whatever.

00:53:39.740 --> 00:53:44.080
And he's seen a lot of athletes.

00:53:44.080 --> 00:53:49.500
And that's the whole idea is that you get
somebody to correct the little bitty movements,

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:53.319
and then you build on the more complicated,
the more demanding movements.

00:53:53.319 --> 00:53:54.319
Yeah.

00:53:54.319 --> 00:53:59.950
Tim Ferriss: Do you have an opinion of, I've
not experienced this personally, but something

00:53:59.950 --> 00:54:00.950
called DNS?

00:54:00.950 --> 00:54:05.079
I think it's dynamic neuromuscular —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Neuromuscular Stabilization.

00:54:05.079 --> 00:54:06.079
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:54:06.079 --> 00:54:11.380
And it seems like they build based on some
sort of motor skill development chronology

00:54:11.380 --> 00:54:12.650
from childhood.

00:54:12.650 --> 00:54:19.950
So you'd start with supine or prone, and then
move to crawling or something like that, quadruped,

00:54:19.950 --> 00:54:21.860
and then kneeling and so on.

00:54:21.860 --> 00:54:24.080
Do you have any perspective on this or could
you —

00:54:24.080 --> 00:54:26.640
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, to be perfectly
honest, I think you're already grown.

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:29.600
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.

00:54:29.600 --> 00:54:30.600
Yep.

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:34.180
Certainly, if my shiny pate is any indication,
then yes.

00:54:34.180 --> 00:54:38.710
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Looking at your well-shaved
head, I'm always reminded of what my father

00:54:38.710 --> 00:54:41.100
used to say who was also bald from an early
age.

00:54:41.100 --> 00:54:44.319
And he would always say "Grass can't grow
on a busy street."

00:54:44.319 --> 00:54:46.339
Tim Ferriss: That's hilarious.

00:54:46.339 --> 00:54:47.869
Definitely going to use that.

00:54:47.869 --> 00:54:49.290
Yeah, I know.

00:54:49.290 --> 00:54:50.290
Yes.

00:54:50.290 --> 00:54:52.430
No, grass cannot grow on a busy street.

00:54:52.430 --> 00:54:56.710
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I don't think you need
to stage it like that.

00:54:56.710 --> 00:55:02.770
That's why I go through the whole exam and
look at the finished product that needs some

00:55:02.770 --> 00:55:10.599
fine-tuning, the thing we talked about in
the beginning, and that movement-causing pathology

00:55:10.599 --> 00:55:15.600
and the fact that it's really your everyday
activities that cause it in the first place.

00:55:15.600 --> 00:55:21.190
And so that's why you've got to change the
way you're doing basic things.

00:55:21.190 --> 00:55:22.780
Do you know what I mean?

00:55:22.780 --> 00:55:23.780
I do.

00:55:23.780 --> 00:55:26.480
And look at that because that's what caused
the problem.

00:55:26.480 --> 00:55:27.700
That's what you need to change.

00:55:27.700 --> 00:55:32.130
Tim Ferriss: What is, I believe you call it
collapso-smasho.

00:55:32.130 --> 00:55:36.760
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Where did you get — I
didn't put that down.

00:55:36.760 --> 00:55:39.420
Well, there are two forms here.

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:43.589
It relates to this fact that what we talked
about already, that the abdominals can get

00:55:43.589 --> 00:55:51.069
— well, that the spine suffers from compression
type things.

00:55:51.069 --> 00:55:54.829
And in the older individual without muscles,
it's collapso-smasho.

00:55:54.829 --> 00:55:59.559
In younger individuals like yourself or others
who have done a whole lot of abdominal exercises,

00:55:59.559 --> 00:56:09.150
I call it squeezo-smasho because the passive
tension from those muscles is adding to the

00:56:09.150 --> 00:56:10.150
compression.

00:56:10.150 --> 00:56:17.069
And the older people are people who are hypermobile,
and it's kind of collapsing down without enough

00:56:17.069 --> 00:56:18.069
support.

00:56:18.069 --> 00:56:22.030
So it's not good if you don't have enough
muscle, and it's not good if you have too

00:56:22.030 --> 00:56:23.190
much muscle.

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:25.049
It's what's in between that's most important.

00:56:25.049 --> 00:56:29.730
Tim Ferriss: So how do you find the Goldilocks
amount of —

00:56:29.730 --> 00:56:32.859
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: No pain and they look
great.

00:56:32.859 --> 00:56:36.260
Tim Ferriss: Keep fine-tuning.

00:56:36.260 --> 00:56:41.579
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And the other thing
is obviously we aren't all built the same.

00:56:41.579 --> 00:56:45.760
I think that's one of the other important
things is looking for structural variations

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:48.609
and building that into the assessment.

00:56:48.609 --> 00:56:51.730
That's the part that's tricky.

00:56:51.730 --> 00:56:54.490
People don't always pick the right parents,
and when they find out, it's too late to go

00:56:54.490 --> 00:56:55.490
back.

00:56:55.490 --> 00:56:58.069
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:56:58.069 --> 00:57:03.870
So let's talk about perhaps other common pathological
patterns.

00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:11.109
I've read that you've said, or written perhaps,
that most people wear their shoulders too

00:57:11.109 --> 00:57:12.819
low.

00:57:12.819 --> 00:57:19.000
Could you speak to that and perhaps also mention
what corrective measures can be taken?

00:57:19.000 --> 00:57:22.020
What does it mean for people to wear their
shoulders too low?

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:27.869
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, it means that
they should sit up at an angle that is about

00:57:27.869 --> 00:57:31.230
six degrees or so higher.

00:57:31.230 --> 00:57:36.020
And often you see when — you don't look
so bad, but when people have done a lot of

00:57:36.020 --> 00:57:40.770
weight training, that's one end of this game,
you'll see that their shoulders look really

00:57:40.770 --> 00:57:41.770
dropped.

00:57:41.770 --> 00:57:42.770
They look lower.

00:57:42.770 --> 00:57:46.890
So at the end of their shoulder —
Tim Ferriss: They look like they're sloped

00:57:46.890 --> 00:57:47.890
downward.

00:57:47.890 --> 00:57:48.890
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

00:57:48.890 --> 00:57:56.010
If you know there's the cervical vertebrae
between C6 and C7, you should have — that's

00:57:56.010 --> 00:57:59.110
about the level where your shoulders should
be.

00:57:59.110 --> 00:58:04.369
And so if that's way down, you can look at
your clavicle and you see that there's not

00:58:04.369 --> 00:58:08.020
this six degree angle, but your shoulder is
lower than your —

00:58:08.020 --> 00:58:09.020
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I see.

00:58:09.020 --> 00:58:12.569
So the clavicle should, from inside to outside,
have roughly a six-degree upward angle.

00:58:12.569 --> 00:58:15.150
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Should have a little
tilt to it, upward angle.

00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:16.150
Right.

00:58:16.150 --> 00:58:17.200
So it could be too low.

00:58:17.200 --> 00:58:23.410
And then the other way I like to talk about
it is just the weight of the world, the husband,

00:58:23.410 --> 00:58:29.119
the children, the bra straps, all of that
pulling down and women's shoulder girdles

00:58:29.119 --> 00:58:30.750
aren't as stiff.

00:58:30.750 --> 00:58:34.650
And what's important about this is it's not
only a factor of what it does to your shoulders,

00:58:34.650 --> 00:58:39.860
it also does a number on your neck because
the muscles that help to hold your shoulder

00:58:39.860 --> 00:58:43.200
blades up attach to your cervical vertebrae.

00:58:43.200 --> 00:58:49.710
And the really big important thing on trying
to address that is using, I think the muscle

00:58:49.710 --> 00:58:52.710
that's named the serratus anterior.

00:58:52.710 --> 00:58:58.510
The serratus anterior attaches to your ribcage
and it can act like a sling, so it attaches

00:58:58.510 --> 00:59:02.559
to your rib cage and to your shoulder blades.

00:59:02.559 --> 00:59:06.520
So it's like a sling that can help to hold
your shoulder blades up and take some of that

00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:12.330
load off of your cervical spine as well as
put your shoulders in the right position.

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:18.170
And having your shoulders sitting at the right
position is important for the glenohumeral

00:59:18.170 --> 00:59:24.369
joint for all of the shoulder joint motions
to work without subjecting them to too much

00:59:24.369 --> 00:59:25.369
injury.

00:59:25.369 --> 00:59:28.869
Tim Ferriss: It seems like perhaps in weight
training, I don't know if this contributes

00:59:28.869 --> 00:59:36.069
to the sloped shoulders and maybe the flat
or downward angle of the clavicle, but the

00:59:36.069 --> 00:59:45.980
advice to depress and retract your shoulders
is common for any number of exercises.

00:59:45.980 --> 00:59:50.270
And I recall meeting with Eric and what he
has a lot of his athletes do, and this is

00:59:50.270 --> 00:59:53.890
very individual dependent, so I'm not making
a blanket recommendation, and I'm not speaking

00:59:53.890 --> 01:00:00.740
for Eric, but for instance, as he has someone
maybe retract on some type of standing, pulling

01:00:00.740 --> 01:00:08.109
motion with say, some type of cable machine,
he'll have them reach forward with the opposing

01:00:08.109 --> 01:00:09.109
side.

01:00:09.109 --> 01:00:15.220
And it seems to me that there's less of that
fixed, depressed and retracted position in

01:00:15.220 --> 01:00:19.700
a lot of what he recommends.

01:00:19.700 --> 01:00:24.900
Is working on the serratus anterior, doing
exercises for the serratus anterior, enough

01:00:24.900 --> 01:00:32.160
to correct that downward sloped angle in people
who have that as a current state of affairs?

01:00:32.160 --> 01:00:36.319
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Number one is also you
have to be sure when people have worked out,

01:00:36.319 --> 01:00:39.080
now it depends upon, again, what their workout
routine is.

01:00:39.080 --> 01:00:45.020
If they're somebody that hangs and does chin-ups,
one of the things can be the latissimus dorsi,

01:00:45.020 --> 01:00:50.590
is this big muscle, and people do lap cool
downs, et cetera, or they do climbing things

01:00:50.590 --> 01:00:55.240
or hanging things, and that muscle will pull
your shoulders down.

01:00:55.240 --> 01:00:59.250
So you've got to make sure that that muscle
hasn't got too short and that you can get

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:01.520
your arms up over your head in the first place.

01:01:01.520 --> 01:01:09.080
Number two is what you really want to do is
use the upper trapezius and the exercises

01:01:09.080 --> 01:01:16.133
where you're down here is using the rhomboids
and the middle trapezius, and those don't

01:01:16.133 --> 01:01:17.329
—
Tim Ferriss: And just for people who are listening,

01:01:17.329 --> 01:01:19.619
this is like a rowing motion, is that what
—

01:01:19.619 --> 01:01:23.800
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, anything where
your arms are below your shoulders or at the

01:01:23.800 --> 01:01:30.080
level of your shoulders, you're using muscles,
they pull your shoulder blades together, but

01:01:30.080 --> 01:01:33.990
particularly the rhomboids downwardly rotate.

01:01:33.990 --> 01:01:38.820
So it's going to make it more difficult to
get your scapula to upwardly rotate and to

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:41.430
get the upper trapezius to work.

01:01:41.430 --> 01:01:48.299
So one of the things that is probably more
effective is actually I like to start people

01:01:48.299 --> 01:01:52.430
off, and I think that's the other thing that
when I look at what recommendations there

01:01:52.430 --> 01:01:55.359
are on the internet, they never show people
where to start.

01:01:55.359 --> 01:01:57.329
It's like, do this exercise.

01:01:57.329 --> 01:02:00.550
Well, not everybody's ready to do that exercise.

01:02:00.550 --> 01:02:03.970
They've got to get ready to do it the way
that's recommended.

01:02:03.970 --> 01:02:11.250
But if you can face the wall and slide your
arms up the wall, and then once your arms

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:16.920
get to shoulder height, particularly when
they're lower, then you shrug a little bit

01:02:16.920 --> 01:02:21.970
to get them up and then you try to lift them
off while holding your shoulders up.

01:02:21.970 --> 01:02:24.310
Tim Ferriss: How far are you standing from
the wall?

01:02:24.310 --> 01:02:26.160
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right up there facing.

01:02:26.160 --> 01:02:27.160
Tim Ferriss: Oh, got it.

01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:28.160
Okay, got it.

01:02:28.160 --> 01:02:29.990
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right up there facing,
your elbows are flexed.

01:02:29.990 --> 01:02:33.960
You're sliding the little finger side of your
hands up there to get it.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:38.190
And then if you just let your shoulders drop,
you've gained nothing.

01:02:38.190 --> 01:02:42.670
But you've got to also hold them up as you
lower your arms, you see.

01:02:42.670 --> 01:02:45.720
Tim Ferriss: Keeping your shoulders up as
you do this exercise.

01:02:45.720 --> 01:02:49.760
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Keep them up, get them
up to where they should be worn, not closer

01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:51.460
to your ears than your iliac crest.

01:02:51.460 --> 01:02:53.150
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:02:53.150 --> 01:02:58.119
So you're looking to keep the shoulders in
the position where the clavicle is angling

01:02:58.119 --> 01:02:59.119
up slightly.

01:02:59.119 --> 01:03:00.119
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Where you want to wear
them.

01:03:00.119 --> 01:03:01.119
Yeah.

01:03:01.119 --> 01:03:04.730
And the other thing is it just makes such
common sense.

01:03:04.730 --> 01:03:08.549
You can't spend the rest of the day have them
hanging down either.

01:03:08.549 --> 01:03:13.520
So you should have a chair that has armrests
on it so that they're up when you're got your

01:03:13.520 --> 01:03:14.770
armrests up.

01:03:14.770 --> 01:03:17.119
If you have to stand a lot, you can put them
on your hips.

01:03:17.119 --> 01:03:22.349
If one shoulder in particular is problematic,
you can support it with the other hand.

01:03:22.349 --> 01:03:26.520
So again, exercises will mean nothing if you
don't follow through.

01:03:26.520 --> 01:03:32.839
If you're doing even 20 minutes of exercise,
but you're spending 12 hours with your shoulders

01:03:32.839 --> 01:03:36.630
hanging down, it isn't not going to work.

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:42.751
Tim Ferriss: What is your position on, and
I know there are many different types, but

01:03:42.751 --> 01:03:46.030
stretching?

01:03:46.030 --> 01:03:52.470
This can be a controversial, sometimes polarizing
topic, but could you elaborate on how various

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:57.339
forms of stretching should or shouldn't be
used in healthy and rehabilitating populations?

01:03:57.339 --> 01:04:01.220
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, number one is
really understanding what you're meaning by

01:04:01.220 --> 01:04:02.250
stretching.

01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:09.050
I've talked about this as we've gone on through
this, but I keep using the term stiffness

01:04:09.050 --> 01:04:15.869
because I feel like I was misled during my
early days as a physical therapist when I

01:04:15.869 --> 01:04:24.930
didn't use my own sense of looking carefully
because we were told that certain movements

01:04:24.930 --> 01:04:29.779
occurred because a muscle was too short and
it needed to be stretched.

01:04:29.779 --> 01:04:31.599
I'll give you an example.

01:04:31.599 --> 01:04:36.200
Well, the example we talked about already,
if you're on your back and you slide your

01:04:36.200 --> 01:04:40.670
legs down and your pelvis tilts forward, your
hip flexors are too short.

01:04:40.670 --> 01:04:46.339
Well, actually what it is is the struggle
between the tension from your abdominals and

01:04:46.339 --> 01:04:49.069
the tension from your hip flexors.

01:04:49.069 --> 01:04:55.040
So stretching your hip flexors is not going
to fix the lack of tension in your abdominals.

01:04:55.040 --> 01:05:00.600
And so what I find is that most often there
aren't muscles that are short, there's a relative

01:05:00.600 --> 01:05:02.980
stiffness problem.

01:05:02.980 --> 01:05:10.809
So improving the stiffness of your abdominals
will elongate your hip flexors.

01:05:10.809 --> 01:05:12.329
Do you see what I mean?

01:05:12.329 --> 01:05:13.329
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:05:13.329 --> 01:05:17.130
It's a relationship, not just an isolated
muscle that you need to stretch.

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:18.130
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right.

01:05:18.130 --> 01:05:21.680
And that it isn't the length of the hip flexors
that's the problem.

01:05:21.680 --> 01:05:26.039
It's the passive tension from those muscles.

01:05:26.039 --> 01:05:32.410
Because when you're sliding your leg down,
that muscle isn't that active anyway.

01:05:32.410 --> 01:05:36.000
And if it's related to the length of the muscle,
it shouldn't occur until you get to the end

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:40.420
of that muscle length, and that's not when
the tilting occurs.

01:05:40.420 --> 01:05:43.140
So there's all these things that just don't
make sense.

01:05:43.140 --> 01:05:49.079
So what I found through these numerous years
of experience I've had is that there's many

01:05:49.079 --> 01:05:55.700
more problems with relative stiffness than
there is with muscles really being short.

01:05:55.700 --> 01:06:03.430
Now, if it's really short, then you also need
to find out what's making it short, because

01:06:03.430 --> 01:06:06.200
it doesn't just like, oh, I think I'll go
short today.

01:06:06.200 --> 01:06:08.770
It's a matter of what is your activity?

01:06:08.770 --> 01:06:12.940
For example, when I'm teaching courses, I
have a great picture of a young man who has

01:06:12.940 --> 01:06:17.700
really short, he has big curve, like you're
talking about, an increased lumbar curve.

01:06:17.700 --> 01:06:21.630
Clearly his hip flexors are short, and he's
a cyclist.

01:06:21.630 --> 01:06:24.770
Well, you usually don't see that kind of alignment
in a cyclist.

01:06:24.770 --> 01:06:27.030
You usually see a flatter back.

01:06:27.030 --> 01:06:31.720
But if I didn't let him use toe clips, he
couldn't move the pedals around.

01:06:31.720 --> 01:06:38.369
So he moved the pedals by flexing rather than
pushing.

01:06:38.369 --> 01:06:41.299
So that's why his hip flexors were short.

01:06:41.299 --> 01:06:46.060
So it wouldn't matter how much you stretched
them, if every time he went out to ride the

01:06:46.060 --> 01:06:50.200
bicycle, he's using his hip flexors all over
again in a shortened position.

01:06:50.200 --> 01:06:51.200
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:55.360
So he's basically in that rear half of the
rotation of the pedal, he's pulling with his

01:06:55.360 --> 01:06:56.360
hip flexors.

01:06:56.360 --> 01:06:57.450
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right.

01:06:57.450 --> 01:07:00.829
Instead of more pushing than just the lifting.

01:07:00.829 --> 01:07:08.610
So what is it that people are doing that's
causing that muscle to get short?

01:07:08.610 --> 01:07:13.309
Other examples, and again, I find it so interesting
about what intensive weight training is doing,

01:07:13.309 --> 01:07:19.369
because if you're lifting, I've actually examined
young women who are lifting twice their body

01:07:19.369 --> 01:07:20.369
weight.

01:07:20.369 --> 01:07:25.590
Well, how many muscles do you use if you're
lifting twice your body weight in a deadlift?

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:27.799
Every little muscle you've got in your body.

01:07:27.799 --> 01:07:33.280
Well, what happens is you end up training
all of those muscles to come on, and they

01:07:33.280 --> 01:07:39.540
don't just go off, so they walk stiff-legged
because there's too much activation of these

01:07:39.540 --> 01:07:40.809
muscles.

01:07:40.809 --> 01:07:46.320
And so they can't stand and have muscles relaxed
because everything comes on.

01:07:46.320 --> 01:07:49.480
It doesn't say, "I'll only come on when I'm
weight training."

01:07:49.480 --> 01:07:57.230
You've trained them to come on, and now you've
got much more output for a given muscle than

01:07:57.230 --> 01:07:58.520
you would otherwise.

01:07:58.520 --> 01:07:59.839
So it's too much.

01:07:59.839 --> 01:08:05.670
So you've got to learn also how to not activate
them as much as you're learning how to activate.

01:08:05.670 --> 01:08:11.170
I'm not against any of this, it's just that
you need to know what all the additional factors

01:08:11.170 --> 01:08:13.690
are that take place with this training.

01:08:13.690 --> 01:08:18.970
So it's a long answer to the question about
stretching, but my big point is you've got

01:08:18.970 --> 01:08:24.730
to figure out what's making it short, that
if you need constant stretching, you've got

01:08:24.730 --> 01:08:28.640
a problem with what's making it short that
has to be addressed.

01:08:28.640 --> 01:08:34.159
Tim Ferriss: So I've been advised, and it
seems to help a bit, but to do a fair amount

01:08:34.159 --> 01:08:42.299
of say, hip bridging or glute activation alternated
with say, hip flexor stretches.

01:08:42.299 --> 01:08:51.550
And if I have, in addition to that, the stiffness
in the abdominal muscles contributing to this

01:08:51.550 --> 01:08:57.739
lower back presentation, these symptoms I'm
experiencing, could you just remind me of

01:08:57.739 --> 01:09:02.060
how I would then work on that abdominal stiffness?

01:09:02.060 --> 01:09:05.069
I could do the overhead breathing and the
side bends.

01:09:05.069 --> 01:09:07.420
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, that's for that
stiffness.

01:09:07.420 --> 01:09:11.411
But again, what you're telling me, and again,
I haven't seen you standing, but —

01:09:11.411 --> 01:09:12.549
Tim Ferriss: I could stand.

01:09:12.549 --> 01:09:13.549
We're also on video.

01:09:13.549 --> 01:09:14.549
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

01:09:14.549 --> 01:09:15.549
Tim Ferriss: Would you like me to stand?

01:09:15.549 --> 01:09:16.760
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, please, please.

01:09:16.760 --> 01:09:17.990
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:09:17.990 --> 01:09:19.440
And I can tilt the camera as needed.

01:09:19.440 --> 01:09:20.750
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

01:09:20.750 --> 01:09:22.060
Pull up your shirt so I can see.

01:09:22.060 --> 01:09:24.310
Tim Ferriss: There we go.

01:09:24.310 --> 01:09:26.120
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

01:09:26.120 --> 01:09:27.319
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:09:27.319 --> 01:09:29.520
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Now turn sideways for
me.

01:09:29.520 --> 01:09:30.520
Oh, yeah.

01:09:30.520 --> 01:09:31.520
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.

01:09:31.520 --> 01:09:32.520
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: No, no, no.

01:09:32.520 --> 01:09:33.520
I mean, I don't know what you're talking about,
your tummy sticking out.

01:09:33.520 --> 01:09:34.609
Tim Ferriss: Oh, well, yeah.

01:09:34.609 --> 01:09:38.270
I mean, if I want to look in a second trimester,
I can do this.

01:09:38.270 --> 01:09:40.450
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Which one's the real
you?

01:09:40.450 --> 01:09:42.430
Tim Ferriss: This is probably the real me.

01:09:42.430 --> 01:09:43.430
This is the real me.

01:09:43.430 --> 01:09:47.659
This is how I would stand if I were standing
at an event, I would probably try to tuck

01:09:47.659 --> 01:09:49.719
my hips a bit to take the pressure off my
back.

01:09:49.719 --> 01:09:53.640
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But you see, you don't
have an increased lumbar curve.

01:09:53.640 --> 01:09:55.150
You've got an increased thoracic curve.

01:09:55.150 --> 01:09:57.199
In other words —
Tim Ferriss: Like the kyphosis on the back,

01:09:57.199 --> 01:09:58.250
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:09:58.250 --> 01:10:01.050
And part of that kyphosis, yeah, right up
there.

01:10:01.050 --> 01:10:05.350
And part of that kyphosis comes from your
rectus abdominus pulling down on your thoracic

01:10:05.350 --> 01:10:06.350
spine.

01:10:06.350 --> 01:10:07.350
Tim Ferriss: Interesting.

01:10:07.350 --> 01:10:08.350
Right here.

01:10:08.350 --> 01:10:09.350
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:10:09.350 --> 01:10:13.190
And see, when you lean back like that then,
well, I'm telling you though, the rectus and

01:10:13.190 --> 01:10:15.580
the other abdominals become your anti-gravity
muscles.

01:10:15.580 --> 01:10:17.199
So they're constantly being used.

01:10:17.199 --> 01:10:19.949
Tim Ferriss: Meaning I lean back and then
these are pulling me forward.

01:10:19.949 --> 01:10:26.210
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, you lean back
to get away from those, but the rectus needs

01:10:26.210 --> 01:10:27.500
to be a little bit longer.

01:10:27.500 --> 01:10:28.699
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:10:28.699 --> 01:10:29.699
This needs to be longer.

01:10:29.699 --> 01:10:33.430
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: To decrease, and the
best way for you to do that is to do the quadruped

01:10:33.430 --> 01:10:35.530
thing and then let your thoracic spine go
down.

01:10:35.530 --> 01:10:38.000
Tim Ferriss: Now, so that would be almost
like the cow?

01:10:38.000 --> 01:10:39.370
No, no, the cat.

01:10:39.370 --> 01:10:40.440
The cat cow, I guess.

01:10:40.440 --> 01:10:42.170
As I'm on my hands and knees —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: All you need to do is

01:10:42.170 --> 01:10:47.170
think about letting your chest go towards
the floor and you feel the load on your shoulder

01:10:47.170 --> 01:10:48.210
blades.

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:49.250
Okay.

01:10:49.250 --> 01:10:53.409
Now let me watch you just easily, like you're
going to contract your tummy to pull it in.

01:10:53.409 --> 01:10:55.510
Yeah.

01:10:55.510 --> 01:10:56.929
You see you do sway back.

01:10:56.929 --> 01:10:58.429
Okay, stop.

01:10:58.429 --> 01:10:59.429
You did lean back.

01:10:59.429 --> 01:11:01.960
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, if I'm going to —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Don't, that's it.

01:11:01.960 --> 01:11:02.960
That's better.

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:03.960
That's better.

01:11:03.960 --> 01:11:05.100
Now, do you have any symptoms like that at
all?

01:11:05.100 --> 01:11:06.620
Tim Ferriss: No.

01:11:06.620 --> 01:11:11.390
There's a little bit of tightness here, but
it actually, it doesn't bother me right now.

01:11:11.390 --> 01:11:12.470
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, okay.

01:11:12.470 --> 01:11:13.470
Yeah.

01:11:13.470 --> 01:11:14.490
And it looks like your hips are fairly straight.

01:11:14.490 --> 01:11:18.570
So go ahead and turn, put your back to me
so I can just, put your back to me for a minute

01:11:18.570 --> 01:11:21.610
and put your hands on your iliac crest.

01:11:21.610 --> 01:11:26.100
Now, with your hands like that, it looks like
they don't look so bad to me.

01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:30.239
Let's do what we talked about before, put
your feet apart and let's just see what happens.

01:11:30.239 --> 01:11:32.310
Does that, if that changes your symptoms at
all?

01:11:32.310 --> 01:11:33.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it seems to.

01:11:33.310 --> 01:11:34.310
Wearing very slippery shoes.

01:11:34.310 --> 01:11:35.310
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Is it better?

01:11:35.310 --> 01:11:36.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's better.

01:11:36.310 --> 01:11:39.360
I would stand, this would help if I was say,
recording a podcast.

01:11:39.360 --> 01:11:46.139
What would help even more is if I put my one
leg up on something, if I stepped on something,

01:11:46.139 --> 01:11:48.800
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Does it matter which
leg?

01:11:48.800 --> 01:11:53.659
Tim Ferriss: Right leg, because where I feel
most of the pain is localized around this

01:11:53.659 --> 01:11:54.730
bony process here.

01:11:54.730 --> 01:11:56.510
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right, right.

01:11:56.510 --> 01:11:57.510
Yeah.

01:11:57.510 --> 01:11:59.300
So that is going to be the tensor on that
side.

01:11:59.300 --> 01:12:00.699
Now let me do one more thing.

01:12:00.699 --> 01:12:01.830
Just bend over and come back up.

01:12:01.830 --> 01:12:04.440
Tim Ferriss: From the side like this?

01:12:04.440 --> 01:12:09.100
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:12:09.100 --> 01:12:10.409
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:12:10.409 --> 01:12:12.860
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Wow.

01:12:12.860 --> 01:12:13.860
Yeah.

01:12:13.860 --> 01:12:17.760
Now you need to work a bit on how you come
back up from forward bending.

01:12:17.760 --> 01:12:21.540
And don't worry about going over that far.

01:12:21.540 --> 01:12:24.600
You need to come up with your hips and less
back.

01:12:24.600 --> 01:12:29.610
As you finish off your back, you sway back
and do too much back extension.

01:12:29.610 --> 01:12:31.713
Tim Ferriss: Oh, when I get to the top, you're
saying?

01:12:31.713 --> 01:12:32.713
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:12:32.713 --> 01:12:33.713
Yeah, about the last 30, 40 percent.

01:12:33.713 --> 01:12:36.800
Tim Ferriss: So hinge more at the hip?

01:12:36.800 --> 01:12:37.800
Is that what you mean?

01:12:37.800 --> 01:12:41.140
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, just think about
going over and coming back up by making your

01:12:41.140 --> 01:12:42.140
hips extend.

01:12:42.140 --> 01:12:43.260
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:12:43.260 --> 01:12:45.840
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Hold it there.

01:12:45.840 --> 01:12:50.410
Now hips, just come back up with hips, hips,
hips, hips, hips.

01:12:50.410 --> 01:12:51.410
Yeah, that was better.

01:12:51.410 --> 01:12:52.710
Tim Ferriss: Better like that.

01:12:52.710 --> 01:12:54.199
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:12:54.199 --> 01:12:57.270
And try not to let your back sway back.

01:12:57.270 --> 01:13:00.730
You use the momentum of your upper back coming
back to finish up.

01:13:00.730 --> 01:13:01.730
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:13:01.730 --> 01:13:02.730
I get a little —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And then you get a little

01:13:02.730 --> 01:13:04.000
extension and that's not good.

01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:05.000
Tim Ferriss: Overextension.

01:13:05.000 --> 01:13:06.000
Yeah.

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:07.000
Right.

01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:08.000
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Right, right.

01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:09.000
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:10.000
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:13:10.000 --> 01:13:11.000
Tim Ferriss: Anything else that I can — yeah,
I mean I can do more.

01:13:11.000 --> 01:13:12.000
Certainly.

01:13:12.000 --> 01:13:13.000
This is helpful.

01:13:13.000 --> 01:13:16.460
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And I think if you can
easily practice, you've got those, the lower

01:13:16.460 --> 01:13:20.060
abdominals or the external obliques, they're
the ones that tilt your pelvis.

01:13:20.060 --> 01:13:25.590
And if you just easily practice tightening
those, but don't work hard at it so that you

01:13:25.590 --> 01:13:26.590
sway back.

01:13:26.590 --> 01:13:29.440
Tim Ferriss: So this is the external obliques.

01:13:29.440 --> 01:13:31.449
And then what was the other musculature you
mentioned?

01:13:31.449 --> 01:13:33.920
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I'm saying don't sway
back.

01:13:33.920 --> 01:13:37.210
Just easily try to tighten them so you get
a little bit of a pelvic.

01:13:37.210 --> 01:13:38.210
Yeah, plenty.

01:13:38.210 --> 01:13:40.830
Now are you good that way?

01:13:40.830 --> 01:13:42.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm good.

01:13:42.710 --> 01:13:43.710
I'm good.

01:13:43.710 --> 01:13:47.060
And this would be in a standing position when
I would have just a little bit of tension

01:13:47.060 --> 01:13:48.060
in the external waist.

01:13:48.060 --> 01:13:52.080
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: If you do that as much
as you can because you see, it eliminates

01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:54.090
your symptoms, that's all you need to do.

01:13:54.090 --> 01:13:57.210
And then work on that little increased thoracic
kyphosis.

01:13:57.210 --> 01:14:01.550
Tim Ferriss: Or I guess reducing this kyphosis
by lengthening —

01:14:01.550 --> 01:14:05.100
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: If you decrease that,
then you won't sway back so much.

01:14:05.100 --> 01:14:06.100
Tim Ferriss: I got it.

01:14:06.100 --> 01:14:10.510
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And if your rectus abdominis
elongates better, you won't have that tendency

01:14:10.510 --> 01:14:11.950
for a thoracic kyphosis.

01:14:11.950 --> 01:14:16.860
Tim Ferriss: And to extend then elongate the
rectus abdominis, for people listening this

01:14:16.860 --> 01:14:22.350
is the six packs, this stuff running down
the front of the abdominal area.

01:14:22.350 --> 01:14:23.360
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I’m having a hard
time hearing you, Tim.

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:24.360
Tim Ferriss: Sorry.

01:14:24.360 --> 01:14:25.360
Yeah, that’s right.

01:14:25.360 --> 01:14:27.550
I forgot about the microphone over here.

01:14:27.550 --> 01:14:36.730
That to elongate the rectus abdominis, I could
get in that quadruped position and basically

01:14:36.730 --> 01:14:40.250
drop my chest to the floor as I'm pushing
my hips backwards if I'm here.

01:14:40.250 --> 01:14:43.909
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Just in that position,
just let it go down.

01:14:43.909 --> 01:14:45.929
Yeah.

01:14:45.929 --> 01:14:49.560
It's amazing how much you can improve your
alignment and it looks like you could change

01:14:49.560 --> 01:14:50.560
pretty readily.

01:14:50.560 --> 01:14:51.639
Tim Ferriss: Okay, great.

01:14:51.639 --> 01:14:56.010
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And the big thing, so
we started off because you were doing the

01:14:56.010 --> 01:14:57.800
bridging exercise.

01:14:57.800 --> 01:14:59.900
I wouldn't be tempted to do that.

01:14:59.900 --> 01:15:06.530
One, you'll do a much better loading of your
gluteal muscles by that bending over and coming

01:15:06.530 --> 01:15:10.060
back up with your hip extensors.

01:15:10.060 --> 01:15:16.389
And if you tighten it there, there's also
a tendency for the glutes to actually posteriorly

01:15:16.389 --> 01:15:19.280
tilt the pelvis, but the spine doesn't go
along for the ride.

01:15:19.280 --> 01:15:22.270
Tim Ferriss: Could you say more about that,
please?

01:15:22.270 --> 01:15:28.120
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, it's like this
is one of my guilt trips because many years

01:15:28.120 --> 01:15:31.440
ago I was working to get the point across.

01:15:31.440 --> 01:15:32.550
I'll tell you my little story.

01:15:32.550 --> 01:15:33.550
Tim Ferriss: Yes, please.

01:15:33.550 --> 01:15:40.100
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I was working with this
older woman, I thought old was my age now.

01:15:40.100 --> 01:15:45.570
And so she did have spinal stenosis and I
was working on sit to stand, so she didn't

01:15:45.570 --> 01:15:48.030
have any symptoms.

01:15:48.030 --> 01:15:51.510
And I had her try to tighten her abdominals
and she was doing pretty good.

01:15:51.510 --> 01:15:56.690
And then I said, "Okay, now let's tighten
your gluteal muscles as you get up."

01:15:56.690 --> 01:16:01.219
Well, she did that and she got pain shooting
down her legs.

01:16:01.219 --> 01:16:08.120
And the reason being that your gluteal muscles,
as you know, attach to the pelvis, so they

01:16:08.120 --> 01:16:13.920
posteriorly tilt the pelvis, but if the spine
doesn't want to go along for the ride, it

01:16:13.920 --> 01:16:19.460
stays there and you get a translation motion
between where pelvis is moving and the vertebrae

01:16:19.460 --> 01:16:20.460
are not moving.

01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:21.460
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:16:21.460 --> 01:16:26.420
The gluteal muscles are basically pulling
the pelvis out from under the spine in a sense.

01:16:26.420 --> 01:16:27.420
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Exactly.

01:16:27.420 --> 01:16:28.420
Exactly.

01:16:28.420 --> 01:16:29.420
Exactly.

01:16:29.420 --> 01:16:30.420
Not a good idea.

01:16:30.420 --> 01:16:31.420
Tim Ferriss: Not a good idea.

01:16:31.420 --> 01:16:34.180
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Now, if your spine moves
easily, then that's all right.

01:16:34.180 --> 01:16:39.760
And you can get the same effect if you would
put your hand where your spine is and tighten

01:16:39.760 --> 01:16:42.080
your glutes, you'll see your spine doesn't
move.

01:16:42.080 --> 01:16:44.920
Do you see what I mean?

01:16:44.920 --> 01:16:47.870
If it wants to go along for the ride, it'll
go along for the ride.

01:16:47.870 --> 01:16:51.780
But the problem is those people where it doesn't
want to go along for the ride and you're going

01:16:51.780 --> 01:16:52.850
to be one of them.

01:16:52.850 --> 01:16:57.112
Tim Ferriss: So in what ways do I need to
be careful then, or I guess —

01:16:57.112 --> 01:16:59.810
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I just don't think that's
a good exercise for you.

01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:00.810
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:17:00.810 --> 01:17:01.810
The bridging?

01:17:01.810 --> 01:17:02.880
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: The bridging.

01:17:02.880 --> 01:17:03.969
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:17:03.969 --> 01:17:11.190
I feel, especially if I do bridging single-leg,
but even double-legged, I remember it's been

01:17:11.190 --> 01:17:15.140
recommended to me by a number of PTs and I've
told them, I'm like, "Guys, this really bothers

01:17:15.140 --> 01:17:16.140
my back."

01:17:16.140 --> 01:17:17.140
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, yeah.

01:17:17.140 --> 01:17:18.140
It's arching your back.

01:17:18.140 --> 01:17:19.140
It's not a good plan.

01:17:19.140 --> 01:17:20.140
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:17:20.140 --> 01:17:21.140
Okay.

01:17:21.140 --> 01:17:22.140
I'll skip those.

01:17:22.140 --> 01:17:23.140
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I would skip those.

01:17:23.140 --> 01:17:24.140
When do you have time to do all this?

01:17:24.140 --> 01:17:25.140
Tim Ferriss: I don't.

01:17:25.140 --> 01:17:26.140
I don't.

01:17:26.140 --> 01:17:30.780
That's honestly, Shirley, been one of the
compounding factors that has been so frustrating.

01:17:30.780 --> 01:17:39.890
It's not only am I getting very often, entirely
different diagnoses, but I also get 37 different

01:17:39.890 --> 01:17:42.120
programs and there's just no way that I can
fit them in.

01:17:42.120 --> 01:17:46.179
And many are probably conflicting also.

01:17:46.179 --> 01:17:52.120
And I've found the movement-focused approach
to make a lot of, at least intuitive sense

01:17:52.120 --> 01:17:53.120
to me.

01:17:53.120 --> 01:17:58.460
If our entire conscious experience of reality
is modeled on a brain that is evolved to move

01:17:58.460 --> 01:18:05.159
us through space, it just seems to make sense
that that is the variable to pay a lot of

01:18:05.159 --> 01:18:10.810
attention to because it's not just a variable,
but a system of systems as you put it, much

01:18:10.810 --> 01:18:11.889
like the immune system.

01:18:11.889 --> 01:18:13.320
So it makes a whole lot of sense.

01:18:13.320 --> 01:18:17.270
Now, you just mentioned older woman, but she's
now my age.

01:18:17.270 --> 01:18:26.770
Would you mind sharing your age, but also
your own self-care, I suppose, routine?

01:18:26.770 --> 01:18:31.390
What do you do to keep as sharp and as in
shape as you are?

01:18:31.390 --> 01:18:33.159
I would love to know more about that.

01:18:33.159 --> 01:18:35.620
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, next month I'll
be 86 years old.

01:18:35.620 --> 01:18:36.620
Tim Ferriss: It's incredible.

01:18:36.620 --> 01:18:38.170
It's just amazing.

01:18:38.170 --> 01:18:40.320
I would never have guessed in a million years.

01:18:40.320 --> 01:18:42.360
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I'm so lucky.

01:18:42.360 --> 01:18:47.610
I'm so fortunate too, because I don't mind
saying both of my parents had dementia and

01:18:47.610 --> 01:18:53.570
I've now exceeded their ages both in life
and in dementia.

01:18:53.570 --> 01:18:55.080
Right now I don't know that I have any.

01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:57.469
Tim Ferriss: I certainly can't, I would not
think you have any.

01:18:57.469 --> 01:18:58.620
Very, very sure.

01:18:58.620 --> 01:18:59.960
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So far, so good.

01:18:59.960 --> 01:19:01.010
Tim Ferriss: So far, so good.

01:19:01.010 --> 01:19:04.739
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: As I told you, that
very early, number one, choosing to be a physical

01:19:04.739 --> 01:19:05.739
therapist.

01:19:05.739 --> 01:19:12.120
And also I was very fortunate because growing
up I refused to grow up and I played sports.

01:19:12.120 --> 01:19:16.480
In my day, it's, when are you going to grow
up and stop all that stuff?

01:19:16.480 --> 01:19:17.989
And that was very good.

01:19:17.989 --> 01:19:21.740
We also didn't have air conditioning, and
we only had one car.

01:19:21.740 --> 01:19:28.000
So instead of paying more for a bicycle than
a car, I had to ride a big old bicycle and

01:19:28.000 --> 01:19:29.330
ride it everywhere.

01:19:29.330 --> 01:19:35.520
And so I happily laid down enough bone and
enough muscle in my early years.

01:19:35.520 --> 01:19:40.969
And then the physician that I encountered
who was part of the Department of Medicine

01:19:40.969 --> 01:19:46.400
at Washington University in St. Louis, his
name was John Holloszy, he started bringing

01:19:46.400 --> 01:19:49.300
in the lifestyle issues.

01:19:49.300 --> 01:19:55.350
And as a physical therapist, even though I
got a PhD in neurobiology because I wanted

01:19:55.350 --> 01:20:03.690
to solve the motor control problems of the
stroke patient, I stayed very physically active.

01:20:03.690 --> 01:20:09.510
I started really running and doing things
when I started my PhD studies, and after encountering

01:20:09.510 --> 01:20:16.700
this physician and learned a bit about nutrition,
breaking all the family tendencies.

01:20:16.700 --> 01:20:22.400
So basically, and then again, learning about
musculoskeletal problems, even though I was

01:20:22.400 --> 01:20:27.140
really interested in working with neurological
patients, people with spinal cord injury,

01:20:27.140 --> 01:20:36.010
head injury, stroke, I had always had this
tendency to look at how people moved.

01:20:36.010 --> 01:20:41.570
And I tried to figure out why they were moving
the way they were moving, et cetera, and got

01:20:41.570 --> 01:20:45.550
involved with musculoskeletal patients and
they started getting better.

01:20:45.550 --> 01:20:47.699
So I had to figure that out.

01:20:47.699 --> 01:20:52.090
And then I applied my own ideas to myself.

01:20:52.090 --> 01:20:58.240
In fact, I don't know how folks who you want
to get at, but there're really some funny

01:20:58.240 --> 01:20:59.240
stories connected with that.

01:20:59.240 --> 01:21:00.240
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.

01:21:00.240 --> 01:21:02.070
Oh, yeah, no, we love funny stories around
here.

01:21:02.070 --> 01:21:06.460
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, and it really
related to doing this quadruped little exercise

01:21:06.460 --> 01:21:15.429
because, so one funny story was, I was really
poor going through getting my PhD because

01:21:15.429 --> 01:21:21.120
I didn't have any income then and was living
off of a minimum amount of money.

01:21:21.120 --> 01:21:25.560
And so I didn't get to buy new clothes very
often.

01:21:25.560 --> 01:21:30.699
And I had a pair of slacks that I was wearing
for a long time, and a friend actually took

01:21:30.699 --> 01:21:32.060
me out to play golf.

01:21:32.060 --> 01:21:36.190
And it was an older woman at that time and
we're out playing golf.

01:21:36.190 --> 01:21:39.469
And she says, "Shirley, you've got your pants
on backwards."

01:21:39.469 --> 01:21:41.050
And I said, "I'm working on my PhD.

01:21:41.050 --> 01:21:45.449
I think I ought to be able to know how to
put my pants on."

01:21:45.449 --> 01:21:50.710
And so we started looking at the darts, and
sure enough, I had them on backwards.

01:21:50.710 --> 01:21:55.469
Well, before my alignment was such that they
looked all right.

01:21:55.469 --> 01:22:00.370
But now that I was doing this quadruped exercise,
they didn't look all right.

01:22:00.370 --> 01:22:05.469
They were looking funny because I had changed
the curves in my back and my buttocks.

01:22:05.469 --> 01:22:09.390
And so that's why she caught the idea that
I wasn't wearing my pants right.

01:22:09.390 --> 01:22:12.070
Tim Ferriss: Which quadruped movements were
these?

01:22:12.070 --> 01:22:16.639
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: It's just the idea of
being in quadruped and just letting your back

01:22:16.639 --> 01:22:20.270
go down and then locking back.

01:22:20.270 --> 01:22:23.560
I tended to sit, ride the bicycle.

01:22:23.560 --> 01:22:30.750
I was a catcher for three different softball
teams, so I was really in a posterior tilt

01:22:30.750 --> 01:22:35.489
with a really flat back, and I had never really
gone the other way.

01:22:35.489 --> 01:22:39.730
Tim Ferriss: And just for people who are listening,
if you don't know, just to imagine, if you

01:22:39.730 --> 01:22:45.690
imagine the pelvis as a bowl of soup, posterior
tilt, you're kind of pouring soup out the

01:22:45.690 --> 01:22:49.560
back of your pelvis,
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Holding it in the middle.

01:22:49.560 --> 01:22:50.560
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:22:50.560 --> 01:22:51.560
Yeah.

01:22:51.560 --> 01:22:52.560
Right.

01:22:52.560 --> 01:22:53.560
Yeah.

01:22:53.560 --> 01:22:54.560
Got it.

01:22:54.560 --> 01:22:55.560
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So your whole back goes
flat then.

01:22:55.560 --> 01:22:57.710
Tim Ferriss: And when you're pushing your
hips back, you were facilitating more of a

01:22:57.710 --> 01:22:59.239
natural curve in the lower back.

01:22:59.239 --> 01:23:03.810
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I was getting my hips
to bend and letting my back go down so I was

01:23:03.810 --> 01:23:09.139
getting more of a curve, and I think also
decreasing a bit of a tendency towards the

01:23:09.139 --> 01:23:10.870
thoracic kyphosis.

01:23:10.870 --> 01:23:16.889
The other thing that was really interesting,
I used to bowl with a bowling ball.

01:23:16.889 --> 01:23:23.330
And while I was in my PhD program, which took
me six years, I didn't have any money to bowl.

01:23:23.330 --> 01:23:28.429
And so when I went and got my bowling ball
out again, I had to change the finger grips

01:23:28.429 --> 01:23:31.409
because I had stretched my finger flexors
out.

01:23:31.409 --> 01:23:34.929
And so the finger grips no longer fit me because
my fingers were longer.

01:23:34.929 --> 01:23:37.290
Tim Ferriss: So could you explain that?

01:23:37.290 --> 01:23:40.730
So your grips had been molded to fingers that
were —

01:23:40.730 --> 01:23:42.650
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, my finger flexors
because yeah, my fingers were always flexed

01:23:42.650 --> 01:23:44.870
from everything I did.

01:23:44.870 --> 01:23:47.070
And I never really thought about stretching
them out.

01:23:47.070 --> 01:23:51.130
And so when you're in quadruped, you would
have stretching those around.

01:23:51.130 --> 01:23:52.130
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:23:52.130 --> 01:23:54.250
Okay, I see.

01:23:54.250 --> 01:23:58.330
So for people who are listening, because we're
making movements with our hands and gestures

01:23:58.330 --> 01:24:01.179
with the hands, instead of being the keyboard
position, we're going to make the video available

01:24:01.179 --> 01:24:03.840
as well but some people will only hear audio.

01:24:03.840 --> 01:24:10.640
So instead of being sort of that keyboard,
hawk talon position, when you're in quadruped,

01:24:10.640 --> 01:24:15.050
as if you were doing a pushup but not that
pushup position, if your hands are flat on

01:24:15.050 --> 01:24:17.670
the floor, then you're going to be stretching
those flexors.

01:24:17.670 --> 01:24:20.429
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Stretching across the
wrist and across the fingers, yeah.

01:24:20.429 --> 01:24:21.530
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, got it.

01:24:21.530 --> 01:24:23.030
So you had to change your bowling ball.

01:24:23.030 --> 01:24:24.030
That's wild.

01:24:24.030 --> 01:24:25.070
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah.

01:24:25.070 --> 01:24:29.369
And then the other thing is I had always worn
my shoes off so that they were going off to

01:24:29.369 --> 01:24:30.369
the side.

01:24:30.369 --> 01:24:38.110
And after I did this few years of this quadruped
rocking, I didn't walk in the same way and

01:24:38.110 --> 01:24:41.960
my shoes weren't worn off to the side from
just walking.

01:24:41.960 --> 01:24:43.139
Tim Ferriss: Interesting.

01:24:43.139 --> 01:24:47.010
So worn off to the side, you mean the shoes
on the inside were worn or on the —

01:24:47.010 --> 01:24:50.440
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Like the outside of
the right and the inside of the left.

01:24:50.440 --> 01:24:56.639
So there were all these little changes that
took place just from improving my alignment

01:24:56.639 --> 01:24:57.639
partly with that.

01:24:57.639 --> 01:25:02.530
So to go on to answer your full question,
so what do I do these days?

01:25:02.530 --> 01:25:04.030
Guess what?

01:25:04.030 --> 01:25:05.949
I still do quadruped.

01:25:05.949 --> 01:25:08.070
I don't go all the way back and sit on my
heels.

01:25:08.070 --> 01:25:13.179
And I also want to tell you about one thing
people need to know about that exercise.

01:25:13.179 --> 01:25:18.580
And then I do a modified, I do pushups, modified
pushups.

01:25:18.580 --> 01:25:24.869
And then in prone, and I think that if you
could do this carefully it'd be good, is in

01:25:24.869 --> 01:25:31.570
prone, I passively, I flex my knee so that
my leg as much as possible is falling on my

01:25:31.570 --> 01:25:32.570
thigh.

01:25:32.570 --> 01:25:33.570
Do you understand?

01:25:33.570 --> 01:25:38.639
Tim Ferriss: So is this similar to what you're
describing, you're laying down on your chest

01:25:38.639 --> 01:25:39.700
—
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, I'm laying down,

01:25:39.700 --> 01:25:45.060
face down, bend my knee, and I try to get
my leg, just one knee, to fall back on my

01:25:45.060 --> 01:25:46.060
thigh.

01:25:46.060 --> 01:25:48.940
Because I don't want to hold it down at 90.

01:25:48.940 --> 01:25:53.620
In other words, if you bend your knee, you
can go to 90 degrees.

01:25:53.620 --> 01:25:57.780
If you go more than 90 degrees, your leg is
falling on your thigh.

01:25:57.780 --> 01:25:59.060
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:25:59.060 --> 01:26:00.060
Right.

01:26:00.060 --> 01:26:01.910
Your lower leg is sort of falling onto your
hamstring.

01:26:01.910 --> 01:26:02.910
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yes.

01:26:02.910 --> 01:26:03.910
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.

01:26:03.910 --> 01:26:04.910
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: That's your thigh.

01:26:04.910 --> 01:26:05.910
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

01:26:05.910 --> 01:26:10.530
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And in that position,
then I lift my thigh off of the floor.

01:26:10.530 --> 01:26:13.540
I do hip extension, but not high.

01:26:13.540 --> 01:26:15.360
There's only 10 degrees of motion.

01:26:15.360 --> 01:26:20.639
But it's a way to stretch, it's a way to use
your gluteal muscle.

01:26:20.639 --> 01:26:23.300
Because if you use your hamstring, you'll
get a cramp.

01:26:23.300 --> 01:26:27.640
You'll get a bad cramp in your hamstring.

01:26:27.640 --> 01:26:31.699
So just a little bit of hip extension to use
my gluteal muscle.

01:26:31.699 --> 01:26:35.020
Tim Ferriss: How many repetitions are you
doing on each side or what is —

01:26:35.020 --> 01:26:39.070
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I just do 10 repetitions
on one side and then 10 repetitions on the

01:26:39.070 --> 01:26:40.070
other.

01:26:40.070 --> 01:26:45.260
Tim Ferriss: And you're doing roughly 10 repetitions
of the quadruped rocking as well?

01:26:45.260 --> 01:26:46.260
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yep.

01:26:46.260 --> 01:26:48.239
Tim Ferriss: I'm not sure if that's the right
amount.

01:26:48.239 --> 01:26:49.949
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: 15 pushups, 15 modified
pushups.

01:26:49.949 --> 01:26:51.050
Modified is your knees are bent.

01:26:51.050 --> 01:26:52.190
I don't go to my toes.

01:26:52.190 --> 01:26:53.270
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:26:53.270 --> 01:26:54.270
Okay.

01:26:54.270 --> 01:26:59.100
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And then still in the
prone position, knees flex to 90 degrees,

01:26:59.100 --> 01:27:08.840
and then I do hip rotation in both directions,
letting my lower leg come in and then go out.

01:27:08.840 --> 01:27:10.350
Come in and go out.

01:27:10.350 --> 01:27:15.810
Tim Ferriss: So just if I can translate, and
please correct me if I'm getting this wrong,

01:27:15.810 --> 01:27:20.030
but you're bending, you're laying on your
chest, one leg bent to 90 degrees and basically

01:27:20.030 --> 01:27:23.560
windshield wiper with that lower leg on each
side.

01:27:23.560 --> 01:27:25.739
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But I do both at the
same time.

01:27:25.739 --> 01:27:27.100
Tim Ferriss: You do both at the same time.

01:27:27.100 --> 01:27:28.100
I see.

01:27:28.100 --> 01:27:34.230
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So they're not quite
— one's maybe 80, the other maybe 70.

01:27:34.230 --> 01:27:37.310
Because it doesn't take all that long.

01:27:37.310 --> 01:27:44.099
And then with my knees extended straight,
with my lower extremities straight, I alternate

01:27:44.099 --> 01:27:48.240
doing hip extension, but I think about using
my gluteal muscle.

01:27:48.240 --> 01:27:50.940
I think about activating my gluteus maximus.

01:27:50.940 --> 01:27:56.730
And again, only about 10 degrees of hip extension.

01:27:56.730 --> 01:28:00.820
And then in that same position I do hip abduction.

01:28:00.820 --> 01:28:05.500
In other words, one leg out to the side 10
times.

01:28:05.500 --> 01:28:11.280
Because you use your gluteus medias and that
better if you're working against gravity and

01:28:11.280 --> 01:28:14.139
extension than you do when you're supine.

01:28:14.139 --> 01:28:17.320
Supine, you tend to recruit the tensor too
much.

01:28:17.320 --> 01:28:20.290
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:28:20.290 --> 01:28:22.160
Got it.

01:28:22.160 --> 01:28:26.320
And is there anything that follows that abductor?

01:28:26.320 --> 01:28:30.790
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, then I go supine,
turnover.

01:28:30.790 --> 01:28:40.909
And I think this is really — I'm pretty
good about not having a kyphosis, but I have

01:28:40.909 --> 01:28:42.530
to adduct.

01:28:42.530 --> 01:28:48.369
In supine, I adduct, pull my shoulder blades
together and slide my arms up over my head

01:28:48.369 --> 01:28:51.820
so that my arms are all the way up over my
head as much as I can.

01:28:51.820 --> 01:28:53.460
And I'm on a hard floor.

01:28:53.460 --> 01:29:00.960
And I'm starting with my hips and knees bent,
arms up overhead, and then slide one leg down,

01:29:00.960 --> 01:29:02.320
slide the other leg down.

01:29:02.320 --> 01:29:07.710
And believe me, for an older person who's
got a tendency towards collapse-o-smash-o,

01:29:07.710 --> 01:29:13.980
really just getting yourself as stretched
out as possible is so important.

01:29:13.980 --> 01:29:15.840
I mean, really.

01:29:15.840 --> 01:29:21.050
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like for me with my kyphosis
that would also be important.

01:29:21.050 --> 01:29:26.730
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: And the biggest worry
is going to be with the older person, that

01:29:26.730 --> 01:29:31.630
if you have a kyphosis, you're not going to
be able to get your arms on the floor all

01:29:31.630 --> 01:29:32.850
the way up over your head.

01:29:32.850 --> 01:29:34.750
And no pain.

01:29:34.750 --> 01:29:39.050
You don't want any pain on top of your shoulder
because that's not going to be a good plan.

01:29:39.050 --> 01:29:45.460
So they may need to have a pillow up there
when they're first starting so their arms

01:29:45.460 --> 01:29:46.860
don't go all the way back.

01:29:46.860 --> 01:29:51.710
Because you want to avoid any kind of pain
on top of your shoulder.

01:29:51.710 --> 01:29:53.850
But happily, I know how to do it.

01:29:53.850 --> 01:29:55.400
I can do it.

01:29:55.400 --> 01:30:00.230
And then I do actively hip and knee flex.

01:30:00.230 --> 01:30:03.410
Bring one knee towards my chest, put it down,
then the other one.

01:30:03.410 --> 01:30:06.420
And 10 times with each leg.

01:30:06.420 --> 01:30:12.360
And then with one foot on the floor, I do
a straight leg raise.

01:30:12.360 --> 01:30:19.540
But I don't tighten all my leg, my thigh muscles
so that my knee is perfectly straight.

01:30:19.540 --> 01:30:23.710
And I turn it out a little bit so that I don't
use the tensor.

01:30:23.710 --> 01:30:28.960
I want it that if I rotate it out, you'll
use the psoas more.

01:30:28.960 --> 01:30:34.000
And I think about tightening my abdominals.

01:30:34.000 --> 01:30:40.370
Because I have had a significant problem,
and I don't want to put too much stress on

01:30:40.370 --> 01:30:43.500
my lumbar spine from the iliopsoas.

01:30:43.500 --> 01:30:46.290
I want to use it, but I want to protect my
back.

01:30:46.290 --> 01:30:48.190
That's why I have one foot on the floor.

01:30:48.190 --> 01:30:49.590
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:30:49.590 --> 01:30:51.050
That's why you're doing one leg at a time.

01:30:51.050 --> 01:30:52.070
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, yeah.

01:30:52.070 --> 01:30:53.380
It's certainly one leg at a time.

01:30:53.380 --> 01:30:58.670
Tim Ferriss: So could you just reiterate why,
so given the past lumbar issue, why you would

01:30:58.670 --> 01:31:03.040
want to engage the psoas instead of the TFL
in this case?

01:31:03.040 --> 01:31:05.179
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Because I think it's
a good muscle to use.

01:31:05.179 --> 01:31:07.350
I need to be able to flex my hip.

01:31:07.350 --> 01:31:13.580
But the tensor, it has a real low threshold
for activation.

01:31:13.580 --> 01:31:16.840
Interestingly enough, if you would scratch
the bottom of your foot, the first muscle

01:31:16.840 --> 01:31:18.460
would go off, will be your tensor fasciae
latae.

01:31:18.460 --> 01:31:19.739
Tim Ferriss: No kidding.

01:31:19.739 --> 01:31:20.739
Wild.

01:31:20.739 --> 01:31:24.920
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: In fact, I think it's
so interesting because I've tried to contact

01:31:24.920 --> 01:31:28.971
the World Health Organization because all
over the world, the tensor is run amok, but

01:31:28.971 --> 01:31:30.550
they don't want to listen to me.

01:31:30.550 --> 01:31:32.010
Tim Ferriss: They're not returning the calls?

01:31:32.010 --> 01:31:33.010
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: No, I know.

01:31:33.010 --> 01:31:37.429
And it is this little bitty wimpy muscle and
you say how can it cause so much trouble?

01:31:37.429 --> 01:31:38.429
But it sure does.

01:31:38.429 --> 01:31:40.830
It plays a role in what happens to the knee.

01:31:40.830 --> 01:31:45.310
It plays a role in what happens to the back
and the hip.

01:31:45.310 --> 01:31:47.949
It's an evil thing.

01:31:47.949 --> 01:31:49.949
Anyway.

01:31:49.949 --> 01:31:57.650
But an interesting thing, one of my colleagues
was doing a study and we actually had a student

01:31:57.650 --> 01:31:59.880
that did not have a tensor fasciae latae.

01:31:59.880 --> 01:32:00.889
Tim Ferriss: No kidding.

01:32:00.889 --> 01:32:06.770
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, we didn't throw
her out of school or anything.

01:32:06.770 --> 01:32:10.540
But anyway, I couldn't wait to do all the
tests that we do to look for the length of

01:32:10.540 --> 01:32:11.610
the tensor.

01:32:11.610 --> 01:32:18.310
And she was a fair athlete, so it wasn't like
it had been sitting not doing anything if

01:32:18.310 --> 01:32:19.310
it was there.

01:32:19.310 --> 01:32:24.989
And I did all the tests and they were negative,
which was kind of supportive to me that indeed

01:32:24.989 --> 01:32:30.580
the tensor does do things that aren't so kind
to the rest of the body.

01:32:30.580 --> 01:32:34.920
So anyway, all that to say that's why when
I do the straight leg raise, I try to laterally

01:32:34.920 --> 01:32:41.600
rotate my hip, because I'd rather use the
psoas than I would.

01:32:41.600 --> 01:32:43.350
And it's not causing any problems.

01:32:43.350 --> 01:32:48.350
Clearly if I thought I was injuring myself,
I would not do that.

01:32:48.350 --> 01:32:49.680
And then I do one other thing.

01:32:49.680 --> 01:32:55.429
With one leg straight and the other foot on
the floor so that my knee is bent, my foot's

01:32:55.429 --> 01:32:58.409
on the floor, I let my leg go out to the side.

01:32:58.409 --> 01:33:03.390
And that's my way of trying to work on controlling
rotation with the trunk.

01:33:03.390 --> 01:33:08.679
My leg goes out to the side, it wants to rotate
your pelvis, but contracting your abdominals

01:33:08.679 --> 01:33:10.440
prevents that rotation.

01:33:10.440 --> 01:33:14.619
So that's another way I'm trying to work my
abdominals.

01:33:14.619 --> 01:33:18.100
And then I stand up.

01:33:18.100 --> 01:33:24.140
I'm so proud of myself because I can get up
from the floor without any difficulty, and

01:33:24.140 --> 01:33:28.510
many people at my age or many years younger
than that can't do that.

01:33:28.510 --> 01:33:33.429
And put my back to the wall and then do what
I was telling you to do, arms up overhead

01:33:33.429 --> 01:33:36.389
and do the little side bend thing.

01:33:36.389 --> 01:33:38.390
Tim Ferriss: And you do this every day?

01:33:38.390 --> 01:33:39.510
Or how many —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I do.

01:33:39.510 --> 01:33:42.280
And I walk three to four miles a day.

01:33:42.280 --> 01:33:43.489
Tim Ferriss: Amazing.

01:33:43.489 --> 01:33:46.160
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Sometimes ride a stationary
bike.

01:33:46.160 --> 01:33:49.850
Tim Ferriss: Well, these are things I'm paying
more and more attention to.

01:33:49.850 --> 01:33:51.150
I'm so impressed.

01:33:51.150 --> 01:33:57.139
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But let me tell you
one thing now, Tim, that the quadruped, as

01:33:57.139 --> 01:34:01.389
much as I love it and I think it's important,
whether you have a shoulder problem, a cervical

01:34:01.389 --> 01:34:07.330
problem, et cetera, is that one of the things
that's a problem though is, and it can be

01:34:07.330 --> 01:34:11.010
for several reasons, but again, the tensor
is one of them.

01:34:11.010 --> 01:34:17.250
If you rock back and your hip medially rotates,
in other words I've found this in some patients

01:34:17.250 --> 01:34:21.640
that as you rock back, your hips should just
flex.

01:34:21.640 --> 01:34:29.790
But if you are monitoring the femur, you can
sometimes see that it medially rotates.

01:34:29.790 --> 01:34:31.050
That is really bad.

01:34:31.050 --> 01:34:36.369
And the reason why it's really bad is because
it's rotating in your knee joint too.

01:34:36.369 --> 01:34:40.530
And that's a good way to set yourself up for
an ACL tear, anterior cruciate ligament.

01:34:40.530 --> 01:34:48.449
And I think people should be monitoring that,
anybody that's doing that.

01:34:48.449 --> 01:34:53.290
One of the things that helps is if you slightly
laterally rotate your hips.

01:34:53.290 --> 01:34:57.530
Many people, particularly men, come with what
we call femoral retroversion.

01:34:57.530 --> 01:34:58.530
Do you know what that is?

01:34:58.530 --> 01:34:59.530
Tim Ferriss: I don't.

01:34:59.530 --> 01:35:00.750
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Okay.

01:35:00.750 --> 01:35:02.460
Well, you probably have it.

01:35:02.460 --> 01:35:05.909
Femoral retroversion — no, it's a structural
variation.

01:35:05.909 --> 01:35:12.410
And so the femur, as you know, has a head
and neck on it.

01:35:12.410 --> 01:35:13.410
It's angled.

01:35:13.410 --> 01:35:16.350
Well, it's also rotated on the shaft.

01:35:16.350 --> 01:35:25.430
And if it's in the ideal world, the average,
not the normal, the average is that that rotation

01:35:25.430 --> 01:35:27.060
is 15 degrees.

01:35:27.060 --> 01:35:32.970
So the head and neck of the femur are pointing
15 degrees forward.

01:35:32.970 --> 01:35:35.910
Now, many men, it's not rotated.

01:35:35.910 --> 01:35:40.590
So what it means is that when you're doing
your hip rotation, you go out a long ways,

01:35:40.590 --> 01:35:41.719
but you don't go in.

01:35:41.719 --> 01:35:44.310
Tim Ferriss: That's true for me, for sure.

01:35:44.310 --> 01:35:47.190
My internal rotation is terrible compared
to my external rotation.

01:35:47.190 --> 01:35:51.150
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But that's because you
came that way and it should never change.

01:35:51.150 --> 01:35:53.699
No, I mean it.

01:35:53.699 --> 01:35:55.360
And men need to know that.

01:35:55.360 --> 01:36:00.270
In fact, that's a problem because if your
tensor is really developed, you could be sitting

01:36:00.270 --> 01:36:02.619
in hip medial rotation when you shouldn't
be.

01:36:02.619 --> 01:36:07.409
And if your glutes are really good, that'll
also immediately rotate your hip when you're

01:36:07.409 --> 01:36:08.630
sitting.

01:36:08.630 --> 01:36:10.060
That's a problem.

01:36:10.060 --> 01:36:17.270
But one of the ways to, if you're trying to
do the quadruped thing, is to turn your hip

01:36:17.270 --> 01:36:22.270
out a little bit when you're in the quadruped
position so your feet would come together

01:36:22.270 --> 01:36:23.270
a little bit closer.

01:36:23.270 --> 01:36:26.720
Tim Ferriss: Right, more of like a wrestling
par terre position, meaning —

01:36:26.720 --> 01:36:27.720
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I don't know.

01:36:27.720 --> 01:36:28.929
I never wrestled.

01:36:28.929 --> 01:36:31.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:36:31.460 --> 01:36:33.840
I can pick up the slack on explaining that.

01:36:33.840 --> 01:36:36.590
That's from —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Not with a referee,

01:36:36.590 --> 01:36:37.590
anyway.

01:36:37.590 --> 01:36:38.590
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:36:38.590 --> 01:36:39.590
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Anyway.

01:36:39.590 --> 01:36:40.590
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:36:40.590 --> 01:36:46.840
So from the feet to the knees, it would just
be making a very, very, very slight V-shape.

01:36:46.840 --> 01:36:50.230
It's not a V-shape, but the lines would converge,
in other words, behind you.

01:36:50.230 --> 01:36:55.910
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Your feet would be a
little bit closer together.

01:36:55.910 --> 01:36:57.300
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

01:36:57.300 --> 01:36:58.420
You're funny.

01:36:58.420 --> 01:37:03.000
All right, so that makes a lot of sense to
me and I'm certainly comfortable with that.

01:37:03.000 --> 01:37:09.190
What would it mean or how would you read the
movement pattern that I have of sitting and

01:37:09.190 --> 01:37:10.950
having my legs sort of splay open?

01:37:10.950 --> 01:37:11.950
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Really good.

01:37:11.950 --> 01:37:15.239
Tim Ferriss: That's also something really
good that alleviates my low back symptoms.

01:37:15.239 --> 01:37:19.719
If I'm sitting in a chair, oftentimes I'll
take my shoes off and fold them up on top

01:37:19.719 --> 01:37:20.719
of the chair.

01:37:20.719 --> 01:37:24.630
Restaurants hate this by the way, so I do
get chastised occasionally.

01:37:24.630 --> 01:37:27.820
But it alleviates some of my lower back issues.

01:37:27.820 --> 01:37:31.550
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: You're getting it fore
and aft.

01:37:31.550 --> 01:37:36.770
Because you're probably in this — this is
one of the syndromes I have of the hip, that

01:37:36.770 --> 01:37:37.770
I've described of the hip.

01:37:37.770 --> 01:37:43.010
Because if your tensors really develop, it's
going to be holding your femur in medial rotation

01:37:43.010 --> 01:37:45.770
when you're standing because it's pulled taught
then.

01:37:45.770 --> 01:37:50.909
If your gluteals are really well-developed,
when your hip's flexed to 90 degrees, they

01:37:50.909 --> 01:37:53.369
become medial rotators too.

01:37:53.369 --> 01:37:56.469
So they're trying to hold your hip in medial
rotation.

01:37:56.469 --> 01:37:58.670
And you're probably getting that twist on
your back then.

01:37:58.670 --> 01:38:01.730
Do you see what I mean?

01:38:01.730 --> 01:38:06.790
So when you laterally rotate your hips, then
you're taking that pull off of them.

01:38:06.790 --> 01:38:08.830
Tim Ferriss: Taking the pressure off of it,
yeah.

01:38:08.830 --> 01:38:13.369
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: You're not getting that
extra pull on your pelvis from those gluteal

01:38:13.369 --> 01:38:15.070
muscles being pulled so taught.

01:38:15.070 --> 01:38:16.260
And that's where you should be.

01:38:16.260 --> 01:38:20.940
That's your normal thing because you have
femoral retroversion.

01:38:20.940 --> 01:38:27.170
That's one of the things that's bad is because
when people go in and they're taught deadlifts

01:38:27.170 --> 01:38:29.640
and they say make your feet point straight
ahead.

01:38:29.640 --> 01:38:34.750
Well, many men in particular shouldn't have
their feet pointing straight ahead because

01:38:34.750 --> 01:38:37.250
they have this femoral retroversion.

01:38:37.250 --> 01:38:43.170
Also, when they do things that rotate, like
play golf, their feet should be turned out.

01:38:43.170 --> 01:38:46.929
Because if they're straight ahead, they're
at the end of their medial rotation range.

01:38:46.929 --> 01:38:47.949
Do you see what I mean?

01:38:47.949 --> 01:38:48.949
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that makes sense.

01:38:48.949 --> 01:38:50.770
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: So then it will be the
knee or the back or the hips that are going

01:38:50.770 --> 01:38:52.160
to go.

01:38:52.160 --> 01:38:54.420
Tim Ferriss: Makes a lot of sense, yeah.

01:38:54.420 --> 01:38:56.159
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: It's also interesting.

01:38:56.159 --> 01:38:57.610
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, super fascinating.

01:38:57.610 --> 01:39:01.260
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, it's like the
backbone is attached to the pelvic bone.

01:39:01.260 --> 01:39:06.429
And I think to me, that's what's so valuable
about being a physical therapist or looking

01:39:06.429 --> 01:39:07.429
at people.

01:39:07.429 --> 01:39:11.460
Because I can't, like an orthopedic surgeon,
just look at the knee or I can't just look

01:39:11.460 --> 01:39:17.119
at the hip because it's the result of all
of these interactions of the body.

01:39:17.119 --> 01:39:18.430
That's what's so important.

01:39:18.430 --> 01:39:24.570
Tim Ferriss: And in a case like mine where
if you look at family photos, especially on

01:39:24.570 --> 01:39:26.949
my mom's side, the feet point way out.

01:39:26.949 --> 01:39:29.290
A lot of the guys stand, they stand like ducks.

01:39:29.290 --> 01:39:36.130
And now at the same time, I've been told,
and I agree with this, that if one were to

01:39:36.130 --> 01:39:43.980
watch me walk, I have, probably because I
have at times the feet pointing out, very

01:39:43.980 --> 01:39:48.960
little sort of glute hamstring assisted hip
extension.

01:39:48.960 --> 01:39:52.270
So I tend to bend at that lower back.

01:39:52.270 --> 01:39:56.810
Maybe I'm misdiagnosing things, but how would
you make sense of that?

01:39:56.810 --> 01:40:01.750
Would it be bad for me to try to point my
feet a little more straight ahead so that

01:40:01.750 --> 01:40:06.290
I get better hip extension using the gluteal
muscles in the hamstrings versus the lower

01:40:06.290 --> 01:40:07.290
back?

01:40:07.290 --> 01:40:09.030
Or is that going to be setting me up for knee
problems?

01:40:09.030 --> 01:40:13.310
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Usually if you're not
using your gluteal muscles and that it's because

01:40:13.310 --> 01:40:17.000
you're swayed back.

01:40:17.000 --> 01:40:20.929
If you’re swayed back, your line of gravity
is behind your hip joint.

01:40:20.929 --> 01:40:25.310
If your line of gravity is behind your hip
joint, you don't need your gluteals, those

01:40:25.310 --> 01:40:26.500
other muscles.

01:40:26.500 --> 01:40:33.800
So if you reduce that kyphosis and get rid
of the, which isn't bad, it's just not helpful

01:40:33.800 --> 01:40:36.770
for what your condition is.

01:40:36.770 --> 01:40:37.770
And then you go forward.

01:40:37.770 --> 01:40:45.480
And then the other thing is if you also push
off, in other words, when you're walking,

01:40:45.480 --> 01:40:49.449
and this could be another way in which you're
reinforcing what your tensor is doing.

01:40:49.449 --> 01:40:55.170
If you tend to walk by pulling your leg through
rather than pushing with your feet and letting

01:40:55.170 --> 01:41:01.400
it swing through, and if you push with your
feet, you'll activate the extensors more.

01:41:01.400 --> 01:41:05.920
So the two things, the things that may be
contributing to your — and I'm not saying

01:41:05.920 --> 01:41:09.820
this is for sure because I'm obviously not
analyzing you.

01:41:09.820 --> 01:41:14.550
But let's say if we paint a scenario that
you're swayed back with a kyphosis, your line

01:41:14.550 --> 01:41:20.350
of gravity is behind your hip joint, then
the gluteals do not have good definition.

01:41:20.350 --> 01:41:24.619
I call it missing for lack of action.

01:41:24.619 --> 01:41:28.210
And so then you pull your legs forward with
your hip flexors.

01:41:28.210 --> 01:41:32.430
You're just reinforcing the overuse of the
tensor.

01:41:32.430 --> 01:41:39.409
But if you decrease your kyphosis, so your
line of gravity is a little more running through

01:41:39.409 --> 01:41:45.300
your hip rather than way behind it, and you
roll over your feet and you push with your

01:41:45.300 --> 01:41:47.679
feet.

01:41:47.679 --> 01:41:48.679
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:41:48.679 --> 01:41:52.300
So you roll over so that you're pushing with
the ball of your foot and your leg swings

01:41:52.300 --> 01:41:55.659
out, you'll use your gluteals more.

01:41:55.659 --> 01:41:57.000
Just pushing instead of pulling.

01:41:57.000 --> 01:41:58.000
Tim Ferriss: Makes perfect sense.

01:41:58.000 --> 01:42:00.610
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: You want to chase your
center of gravity, not pull it.

01:42:00.610 --> 01:42:06.210
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, this kyphosis has been
with me since I was a little kid.

01:42:06.210 --> 01:42:11.920
I've tried foam rolling, manual release, strengthening
the mid-back.

01:42:11.920 --> 01:42:14.560
But I have not worked on rectus abdominis.

01:42:14.560 --> 01:42:18.920
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: If you were little — there's
a condition called Scheuermann's disease,

01:42:18.920 --> 01:42:21.250
which isn't really a disease.

01:42:21.250 --> 01:42:27.861
But if you had it, particularly it happens
in your teenage years where you get a — you've

01:42:27.861 --> 01:42:28.861
heard of it.

01:42:28.861 --> 01:42:30.560
Tim Ferriss: No, I haven't heard of Scheuermann's
disease.

01:42:30.560 --> 01:42:35.550
But ever since, I would say since I've been
12, 13, I've had this kyphosis lordosis combo.

01:42:35.550 --> 01:42:37.910
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well then you've probably
got Scheuermann's disease.

01:42:37.910 --> 01:42:39.270
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:42:39.270 --> 01:42:40.590
I'll have to look it up.

01:42:40.590 --> 01:42:46.080
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: It's S-C-H-E-U-E-R-M-A-N-N.

01:42:46.080 --> 01:42:50.600
And it's kind of a idiopathic compression
fracture of the thoracic spine.

01:42:50.600 --> 01:42:52.119
It means you won't get rid of it.

01:42:52.119 --> 01:42:53.119
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man.

01:42:53.119 --> 01:42:54.119
Okay.

01:42:54.119 --> 01:42:57.179
Well, can I do something to —
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: But I think if you just

01:42:57.179 --> 01:42:58.599
don't sway back more.

01:42:58.599 --> 01:43:00.750
You know what I mean?

01:43:00.750 --> 01:43:02.730
Just stay forward a little bit.

01:43:02.730 --> 01:43:04.219
If you can make peace with that.

01:43:04.219 --> 01:43:08.200
Tim Ferriss: Well, I can try to work on the
elongating of the —

01:43:08.200 --> 01:43:09.340
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, it's just not
going to change.

01:43:09.340 --> 01:43:10.340
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:43:10.340 --> 01:43:12.659
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: The big thing is don't
let it get worse.

01:43:12.659 --> 01:43:13.773
Tim Ferriss: If I have it, yeah.

01:43:13.773 --> 01:43:14.773
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: If that's what you have.

01:43:14.773 --> 01:43:15.773
Tim Ferriss: So what are the main steps to
—

01:43:15.773 --> 01:43:17.369
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I'm not saying that's
what you have, but I'm saying.

01:43:17.369 --> 01:43:19.159
But if you —
Tim Ferriss: It's possible.

01:43:19.159 --> 01:43:21.050
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Yeah, because it happens
around the teenage years.

01:43:21.050 --> 01:43:25.770
Tim Ferriss: So what would be the keys to
not letting it get worse, would you say?

01:43:25.770 --> 01:43:28.949
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Don't have an increase.

01:43:28.949 --> 01:43:34.270
You can still do the same things, Tim, but
just don't say "I will be absolutely perfect.

01:43:34.270 --> 01:43:37.560
I have to settle for kind-of-perfect instead
of absolute perfect."

01:43:37.560 --> 01:43:43.300
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's probably a good
MO for most of my life, I would say.

01:43:43.300 --> 01:43:46.260
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, most of us don't
get that close.

01:43:46.260 --> 01:43:47.490
There we go.

01:43:47.490 --> 01:43:51.210
Tim Ferriss: Probably further away than I
would like to admit.

01:43:51.210 --> 01:43:54.790
So Shirley, this has been such a great conversation.

01:43:54.790 --> 01:43:55.970
We've covered so much.

01:43:55.970 --> 01:43:59.130
I've taken copious, copious notes.

01:43:59.130 --> 01:44:03.670
And certainly people can find your books,
the Diagnosis and Treatment of Movement Impairment

01:44:03.670 --> 01:44:08.520
Syndromes, which has been translated into
seven languages, as well as your second book,

01:44:08.520 --> 01:44:12.330
Movement System Impairment Syndromes of the
Extremities, Cervical and Thoracic Spines,

01:44:12.330 --> 01:44:16.520
has been very influential in promoting movement
diagnoses.

01:44:16.520 --> 01:44:20.690
Is there anything else you would like to mention?

01:44:20.690 --> 01:44:22.199
Anything else we should talk about?

01:44:22.199 --> 01:44:25.480
Anything you would like to draw attention
to with my audience?

01:44:25.480 --> 01:44:31.760
Anything at all that you think is worth saying
or discussing before we begin to wind to a

01:44:31.760 --> 01:44:32.760
close?

01:44:32.760 --> 01:44:35.420
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Well, it's probably
just a little repeat of all the things we've

01:44:35.420 --> 01:44:36.710
been talking about.

01:44:36.710 --> 01:44:43.150
It's been really great and generous of you
to allow me to discuss your issues.

01:44:43.150 --> 01:44:48.449
But what's really nice is here you are, somebody
that's worked so hard to address all of these

01:44:48.449 --> 01:44:51.970
things with all the discipline that most people
don't have.

01:44:51.970 --> 01:44:57.260
And it's still hard to get a straightforward
story about what's going on and how best to

01:44:57.260 --> 01:44:58.260
suture.

01:44:58.260 --> 01:45:03.240
And that's what worries me a lot is, number
one, is I look on the internet for exercises

01:45:03.240 --> 01:45:06.650
and most of them people can't do.

01:45:06.650 --> 01:45:10.210
And they're not taking into account the variations
in how people are.

01:45:10.210 --> 01:45:14.670
Here you've been through all this exam and
nobody said you have femoral retroversion,

01:45:14.670 --> 01:45:17.489
which you need to know because you came that
way.

01:45:17.489 --> 01:45:20.389
You need to stay that way.

01:45:20.389 --> 01:45:28.530
So I would like to see that there's more respect
for how difficult exercise is.

01:45:28.530 --> 01:45:33.510
It's not like here's the way everybody should
go out and do this one thing and then all

01:45:33.510 --> 01:45:35.010
will be well.

01:45:35.010 --> 01:45:41.219
I'd like to see this recognition of that movement
is as complicated as anything else that the

01:45:41.219 --> 01:45:46.429
body does, that there is a movement system,
a physiological thing.

01:45:46.429 --> 01:45:51.969
And that we should have diagnostic categories
so that people, when they're consulting a

01:45:51.969 --> 01:45:55.750
physical therapist, get a diagnosis just like
when they go to any doctor.

01:45:55.750 --> 01:46:00.030
That's, to me, why you go to a doctor is to
get a diagnosis or find out what condition

01:46:00.030 --> 01:46:02.139
you're working on.

01:46:02.139 --> 01:46:09.570
And I think that would do a lot to help reducing
all this variability in treatment.

01:46:09.570 --> 01:46:14.010
And I think helping people to understand how
it's the way they do their everyday activities

01:46:14.010 --> 01:46:20.119
that causes the problem and that those can
be changed with good direction.

01:46:20.119 --> 01:46:22.400
I can't help but say this too.

01:46:22.400 --> 01:46:26.970
I help a good friend who's actually on a dementia
floor.

01:46:26.970 --> 01:46:31.110
I go to help feed her twice a day.

01:46:31.110 --> 01:46:36.360
And I look at all the other people, older
people that are in assisted living, and the

01:46:36.360 --> 01:46:40.430
majority of them are there because they have
physical disabilities.

01:46:40.430 --> 01:46:43.310
I'm older than most of them.

01:46:43.310 --> 01:46:48.640
And I think if people had a chance to address
these things early on with the discipline

01:46:48.640 --> 01:46:55.730
like you show and good direction, we could
cut down on and improve the quality of life.

01:46:55.730 --> 01:47:05.630
I know you've interviewed Dr. Attia and much
of what he says, Medicine 3.0 or something,

01:47:05.630 --> 01:47:11.260
and if we did that with more care on exercise
and knowing how to do everyday activities

01:47:11.260 --> 01:47:18.270
and not just take them for granted, I think
people could have longer, fuller lives and

01:47:18.270 --> 01:47:19.849
be as fortunate as I am.

01:47:19.849 --> 01:47:21.730
Tim Ferriss: Agreed.

01:47:21.730 --> 01:47:22.989
Agreed, agreed.

01:47:22.989 --> 01:47:25.489
What a great conversation.

01:47:25.489 --> 01:47:28.070
I've learned so much and I've taken so many
notes.

01:47:28.070 --> 01:47:29.949
I have a lot to dig into.

01:47:29.949 --> 01:47:38.679
It's also given me some renewed optimism in
terms of exercises that I can work with, movements

01:47:38.679 --> 01:47:40.550
I should say that I can experiment with.

01:47:40.550 --> 01:47:45.730
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: I hope you'll get back
to me if I can help further.

01:47:45.730 --> 01:47:49.800
If I find out that these things are going
along, I would be more than happy to.

01:47:49.800 --> 01:47:50.840
Tim Ferriss: Thank you.

01:47:50.840 --> 01:47:53.130
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: Just a little more formally.

01:47:53.130 --> 01:47:54.780
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely.

01:47:54.780 --> 01:47:55.909
I appreciate it.

01:47:55.909 --> 01:47:58.239
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: We can do it on this.

01:47:58.239 --> 01:48:00.949
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I would very much like
to do that.

01:48:00.949 --> 01:48:07.250
So thank you for the very kind offer and thank
you for so kindly taking the time to have

01:48:07.250 --> 01:48:08.250
this conversation.

01:48:08.250 --> 01:48:09.469
I think it'll be really helpful to people.

01:48:09.469 --> 01:48:12.520
Dr. Shirley Sahrmann: As my dear friend Michael
said, you are tremendous.

01:48:12.520 --> 01:48:16.869
I've really enjoyed talking to you, and you
do have a sense of enthusiasm on how to ask

01:48:16.869 --> 01:48:19.909
the right questions, to make it fun for both
of us.

01:48:19.909 --> 01:48:20.949
Tim Ferriss: Thank you.

01:48:20.949 --> 01:48:21.969
Thank you so much.

01:48:21.969 --> 01:48:26.599
And for everybody listening, we will link
in the show notes to everything we discussed

01:48:26.599 --> 01:48:29.989
as usual at tim.blog/podcast.

01:48:29.989 --> 01:48:34.810
And until next time, be just a bit kinder
than is necessary, not only to others, but

01:48:34.810 --> 01:48:36.300
also to yourself.

01:48:36.300 --> 01:48:38.489
And as always, thanks for tuning in.

